1
00:00:02,119 --> 00:00:04,921
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm actually especially interested in that you said that, eh...

2
00:00:07,843 --> 00:00:08,324
[SPEAKER_02]: I am.

3
00:00:08,564 --> 00:00:16,250
[SPEAKER_02]: I am actually staying actually as background, but that is what I can't see usually, but when it appears, I am.

4
00:00:16,350 --> 00:00:20,513
[SPEAKER_02]: Actually, that's not the line, that really unhoused, I am.

5
00:00:21,293 --> 00:00:25,156
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the sense of self, it never appears directionally.

6
00:00:26,649 --> 00:00:32,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if... if it were to appear directly, then you can just say, ah, this is not myself, and that would be the end of it.

7
00:00:33,273 --> 00:00:43,540
[SPEAKER_01]: But as... like in the suttas, the Buddha say, Putujana takes... with not-self, he takes his self, or with self, takes the not-self, or with self, he takes the self.

8
00:00:44,220 --> 00:00:47,823
[SPEAKER_01]: But he can never, with not-self, take the not-self.

9
00:00:47,943 --> 00:00:51,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, whatever Putujana looks, self will be there, one way or the other.

10
00:00:53,106 --> 00:00:54,687
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, when people...

11
00:00:57,193 --> 00:01:02,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to apply the practice and say, ah, not myself, not myself, to everything.

12
00:01:03,677 --> 00:01:13,123
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't work, because it's just like a blanket attitude, that you apply to everything, but that means your sense of self is actually left out, and doing that, applying.

13
00:01:14,043 --> 00:01:23,729
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why in the suttas it says, all determinations are impermanent, all determinations are suffering, all things are not self.

14
00:01:24,635 --> 00:01:28,497
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't say all things are impermanent, all things are suffering, all things are not self.

15
00:01:29,057 --> 00:01:30,138
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what most people do.

16
00:01:30,518 --> 00:01:33,299
[SPEAKER_01]: They say, ah, everything is impermanent, everything is not myself.

17
00:01:34,360 --> 00:01:35,340
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't work like that.

18
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:36,201
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, that's true.

19
00:01:36,361 --> 00:01:37,702
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not incorrect to say that.

20
00:01:38,122 --> 00:01:39,563
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not open-eyed.

21
00:01:39,603 --> 00:01:40,383
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't lead on.

22
00:01:40,703 --> 00:01:42,284
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't bring understanding of the dhamma.

23
00:01:42,924 --> 00:01:46,846
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the person needs to include himself into his insight.

24
00:01:48,007 --> 00:01:54,270
[SPEAKER_01]: And doing that is seeing that yourself, your sense of self, depends upon something that is not yours.

25
00:01:55,430 --> 00:02:04,055
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you see that your self directly stands upon something that is not yours, then, automatically, that sense of self is not yours either.

26
00:02:04,595 --> 00:02:08,057
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't have to then, okay, now I will say to myself, this is not myself.

27
00:02:08,077 --> 00:02:08,257
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

28
00:02:08,617 --> 00:02:15,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Your experience, then, is the experience of not-self, if you see determinations of that self as impermanent and as suffering.

29
00:02:16,501 --> 00:02:21,403
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why I'm saying, like, it's not, it's not, it's not incorrect to say, oh, nothing is myself.

30
00:02:21,423 --> 00:02:22,664
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, nothing can be yourself.

31
00:02:23,258 --> 00:02:25,020
[SPEAKER_01]: But in terms of the practice, it just doesn't work.

32
00:02:25,080 --> 00:02:27,362
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, it can work in terms of making people feel better.

33
00:02:29,404 --> 00:02:32,447
[SPEAKER_01]: It's almost like a form of denial, just saying everything is not myself.

34
00:02:33,768 --> 00:02:37,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why it's more important to first recognize that there is a sense of self.

35
00:02:38,893 --> 00:02:43,497
[SPEAKER_01]: I can never see directly, I can never take it up, I can never say this is not mine and be finished with it.

36
00:02:44,298 --> 00:02:48,682
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to recognize that it's there and then affect your whole experience

37
00:02:50,096 --> 00:02:54,739
[SPEAKER_01]: In your practice, through seeing determination, so then, the whole experience will be experience of not-self.

38
00:02:56,279 --> 00:02:59,441
[SPEAKER_01]: But not just selectively pick and choose.

39
00:02:59,461 --> 00:03:09,247
[SPEAKER_01]: So, everything needs to be effective, because although you might not be able to pinpoint the sense of self, you know for sure that it's within the experience as a whole.

40
00:03:09,647 --> 00:03:15,410
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you abandon the experience as a whole, you included... that abandoning includes the abandoning of the sense of self.

41
00:03:16,310 --> 00:03:18,992
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that was the whole... the whole talking about the...

42
00:03:19,446 --> 00:03:21,748
[SPEAKER_01]: The general background and particular intentions.

43
00:03:22,549 --> 00:03:26,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're just going out of particular intention, means you don't really see what's in the background.

44
00:03:28,114 --> 00:03:32,959
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if you don't even see what's in the background, you don't even know how to look for the background.

45
00:03:33,019 --> 00:03:37,723
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever you look for the background, you look as if it's particular.

46
00:03:38,264 --> 00:03:44,069
[SPEAKER_01]: You just make it actual, make it right in front of you, which means something else is a background, which means your sense of self is hiding somewhere else now.

47
00:03:45,470 --> 00:03:51,194
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to learn to be mindful of the general background without over-intending it and making it into particular.

48
00:03:54,336 --> 00:03:55,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's general.

49
00:03:56,457 --> 00:03:59,899
[SPEAKER_01]: And if that's not general, something else will be general.

50
00:04:00,140 --> 00:04:04,302
[SPEAKER_01]: So, learning to do that means, ah, you start to notice more peripherally.

51
00:04:04,843 --> 00:04:10,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Without moving your vision left or right, up or down, you just start to include more in what you're already seeing.

52
00:04:12,087 --> 00:04:14,449
[SPEAKER_01]: But if each time you want to see something, you keep moving,

53
00:04:15,140 --> 00:04:18,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Around, you'll end up seeing only that which you move to.

54
00:04:19,444 --> 00:04:20,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing around it.

55
00:04:20,244 --> 00:04:20,725
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing more.

56
00:04:23,307 --> 00:04:28,231
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that was the whole point of that email and of the sense of I am.

57
00:04:28,711 --> 00:04:31,173
[SPEAKER_02]: What did you mean of the intentional situation?

58
00:04:31,914 --> 00:04:35,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, even that general background is already intentional.

59
00:04:36,137 --> 00:04:39,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, the intentions are not as defined as they are particularly.

60
00:04:40,841 --> 00:04:44,584
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I told you then before, a general situation being in this room,

61
00:04:45,303 --> 00:04:55,528
[SPEAKER_01]: It gives you a certain set of particular intentions as possibilities that are allowable in this room.

62
00:04:55,728 --> 00:05:05,272
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example, if you go outside, you won't have a chance, you won't be able to intend to use this water filter to drink water, because that's in here.

63
00:05:05,967 --> 00:05:07,628
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your general situation has changed.

64
00:05:08,168 --> 00:05:10,489
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now, okay, drinking water is important.

65
00:05:10,909 --> 00:05:17,711
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you start modifying your general situation so that particular intentions can arise where you can get the water to drink.

66
00:05:19,812 --> 00:05:24,054
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not that intentions only come from you.

67
00:05:24,654 --> 00:05:35,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when you think you're intending, the only reason you're able to intend is because your general situation allows you those possibilities that now you are attending and they become intentions.

68
00:05:37,177 --> 00:05:39,460
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't create anything, in other words.

69
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:40,341
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't come up.

70
00:05:40,941 --> 00:05:47,709
[SPEAKER_01]: Because even if you come up with new things, an intention of a new thing is given from their general background.

71
00:05:49,291 --> 00:05:50,332
[SPEAKER_01]: So you can only use

72
00:05:51,468 --> 00:06:12,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Or choose between the given set of possibilities based on the general situation, where you are, your abilities, your... and, like, body and bodily predispositions, height, strength, eh... complexion, all of those are form and they partake, or rather, they're fundamental form of a general situation.

73
00:06:12,999 --> 00:06:20,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, a short, eh... disabled person cannot have intentions of running marathon.

74
00:06:21,199 --> 00:06:22,160
[SPEAKER_01]: You might think about it.

75
00:06:22,780 --> 00:06:29,143
[SPEAKER_01]: But for him, it's inherently removed from his fundamental situation that's determined by his body.

76
00:06:29,303 --> 00:06:31,504
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, he can use different set.

77
00:06:31,644 --> 00:06:36,547
[SPEAKER_01]: He can focus on different possibilities that arise and then pursue those directions.

78
00:06:36,607 --> 00:06:41,890
[SPEAKER_01]: But either way, all he can do is kind of modify things that are given beforehand.

79
00:06:42,110 --> 00:06:43,090
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't create anything.

80
00:06:43,771 --> 00:06:44,411
[SPEAKER_01]: And nobody can.

81
00:06:45,412 --> 00:06:47,694
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, yeah.

82
00:06:48,394 --> 00:06:51,836
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's not that, kind of, your choice is given for you.

83
00:06:52,136 --> 00:06:53,937
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is a responsibility for the choice.

84
00:06:54,418 --> 00:06:59,481
[SPEAKER_01]: But the possibilities of your choice are given beforehand.

85
00:06:59,501 --> 00:07:04,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, when you choose between yes and no, well, yes and no are given beforehand as a form of choice.

86
00:07:05,104 --> 00:07:07,626
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you choose one on account of the other.

87
00:07:09,147 --> 00:07:11,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's basically your intentionality that chooses.

88
00:07:11,628 --> 00:07:12,509
[SPEAKER_01]: But by attending

89
00:07:13,789 --> 00:07:16,510
[SPEAKER_01]: By attending the reason for choosing yes, you choose yes.

90
00:07:17,371 --> 00:07:19,372
[SPEAKER_01]: By attending the reason to choose no, you choose no.

91
00:07:20,232 --> 00:07:22,833
[SPEAKER_01]: By, you know, giving in to the external pressure or whatever.

92
00:07:23,113 --> 00:07:25,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Various factors that affect your choice.

93
00:07:26,195 --> 00:07:26,855
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how it works.

94
00:07:27,455 --> 00:07:30,557
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not your me that is... because arahant still has a choice.

95
00:07:31,037 --> 00:07:31,137
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

96
00:07:31,757 --> 00:07:33,598
[SPEAKER_01]: But he doesn't have a sense of self anymore.

97
00:07:34,279 --> 00:07:39,541
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, sense of self interferes and takes credit for these things that happen by himself.

98
00:07:41,142 --> 00:07:41,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Hmm.

99
00:07:42,679 --> 00:07:49,763
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's important to first see that, and then take responsibility for your choice, as if it is you who is choosing it.

100
00:07:50,484 --> 00:07:54,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the only way you can actually, through that, abandon the sense of self.

101
00:07:55,247 --> 00:07:59,390
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't see that, and then immediately start saying, well, I haven't...

102
00:07:59,470 --> 00:08:01,611
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not doing anything, it's just this body and everything.

103
00:08:01,851 --> 00:08:02,632
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the denial.

104
00:08:03,412 --> 00:08:04,793
[SPEAKER_01]: For Putrajana, that's the denial.

105
00:08:05,313 --> 00:08:07,795
[SPEAKER_01]: So Putrajana first has to admit that he is,

106
00:08:08,498 --> 00:08:19,147
[SPEAKER_01]: That he's choosing, that he's responsible, that there are results of his actions, that there is mother, there is father, there is killing, there is giving life, there is all of that, like the mundane right view the Buddha talks about.

107
00:08:19,827 --> 00:08:25,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Once he's able to admit that, to see that, then he can begin abandoning sense of self through that.

108
00:08:25,592 --> 00:08:29,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Without abandoning that view, he abandons his sense of self based on that.

109
00:08:30,897 --> 00:08:37,342
[SPEAKER_01]: But usually people kind of take a shortcut, and then they they go straight into saying, well, everything is not self,

110
00:08:37,853 --> 00:08:45,815
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nobody's doing any action anyway, and so on and so forth, which is actually similar to the wrong views of the Buddha's time that he was often discussing.

111
00:08:46,495 --> 00:08:52,057
[SPEAKER_01]: When people would say, well, there is no door, there is no good or bad, it's just elements and so on and so forth.

112
00:08:52,617 --> 00:08:55,557
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason why that is wrong is because there is a sense of self.

113
00:08:56,498 --> 00:08:58,858
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a mistake, but it's real as a mistake.

114
00:08:59,878 --> 00:09:04,700
[SPEAKER_01]: So, denying a mistake means you'll never be able to solve the mistake, to correct it, to rectify it.

115
00:09:05,140 --> 00:09:05,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Could you deny it?

116
00:09:06,973 --> 00:09:10,674
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first, you have to admit that there is a mistake, and then you'll be able to fix it.

117
00:09:11,695 --> 00:09:24,259
[SPEAKER_02]: But, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but, but,

118
00:09:36,243 --> 00:09:37,664
[SPEAKER_01]: All of those things still stay.

119
00:09:39,305 --> 00:09:43,067
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the ownership is a confusion, thinking that it's me, it's mine, I'm doing it.

120
00:09:46,069 --> 00:09:52,233
[SPEAKER_02]: It's very much a different point, because, as I observe, I think it's a choice, a choice, A or B.

121
00:09:52,933 --> 00:09:53,574
[SPEAKER_02]: They are given.

122
00:09:54,454 --> 00:09:59,377
[SPEAKER_02]: But which one I choose, that one actually appears as thought.

123
00:09:59,738 --> 00:10:00,098
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

124
00:10:00,498 --> 00:10:03,460
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you are responsible for accepting that thought.

125
00:10:04,698 --> 00:10:08,339
[SPEAKER_01]: As a decision, as a choice.

126
00:10:08,359 --> 00:10:09,739
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, the thought appeared.

127
00:10:09,759 --> 00:10:10,900
[SPEAKER_01]: You didn't create it either.

128
00:10:11,780 --> 00:10:17,021
[SPEAKER_01]: But you do choose, you do accept to go with it or not go with it.

129
00:10:19,262 --> 00:10:19,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see what I mean?

130
00:10:20,482 --> 00:10:23,763
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if the thought appears, you can't say the choice has been made for me.

131
00:10:23,883 --> 00:10:24,083
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

132
00:10:24,163 --> 00:10:25,284
[SPEAKER_01]: You always make the choice.

133
00:10:26,684 --> 00:10:26,924
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

134
00:10:27,264 --> 00:10:33,426
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by doing that, by recognizing that, then you can disown yourself making the choice.

135
00:10:33,936 --> 00:10:36,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Not deny yourself making the choice.

136
00:10:36,618 --> 00:10:37,739
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a big difference there.

137
00:10:38,519 --> 00:10:40,641
[SPEAKER_01]: And the difference is in that order of things.

138
00:10:40,741 --> 00:10:46,865
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you admit that there is a mistake, and then, through the admittance of mistake, you can rectify the mistake.

139
00:10:47,646 --> 00:10:49,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the second, the denial, is different.

140
00:10:49,487 --> 00:10:52,890
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically, you see there is a mistake, and then, you deny the mistake.

141
00:10:52,910 --> 00:10:58,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, ah, it's not mine, doesn't exist, it's all fixed already, not a problem, we're all in nibbāna, or whatever.

142
00:10:59,154 --> 00:11:00,555
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you don't see the mistake anymore.

143
00:11:00,575 --> 00:11:02,757
[SPEAKER_01]: You think you fixed it, but actually, you just obscured it

144
00:11:04,661 --> 00:11:12,865
[SPEAKER_01]: So, taking up responsibility for your choice, like really feeling responsible, like, I am doing this, I am choosing this, that is the first step.

145
00:11:14,807 --> 00:11:20,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Through that, you can abandon sense of I, without abandoning responsibility for the action.

146
00:11:20,510 --> 00:11:22,671
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you overcome the action, finally.

147
00:11:23,312 --> 00:11:29,715
[SPEAKER_01]: But for as long as you're avoiding responsibility for it, you're avoiding the nature of action, which means you're not overcoming the khandhā.

148
00:11:41,025 --> 00:11:43,707
[SPEAKER_01]: The choice is there, and there is a mistake of ownership.

149
00:11:44,027 --> 00:11:45,947
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's real, as such.

150
00:11:46,368 --> 00:11:47,248
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to accept it.

151
00:11:47,888 --> 00:11:50,229
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you'll be able to fix it.

152
00:11:52,450 --> 00:11:57,492
[SPEAKER_02]: I was thinking that karma, how do you view in the case of Putrajana, actually?

153
00:11:57,692 --> 00:12:03,175
[SPEAKER_02]: Of course, in the case of Putrajana, of course, that I am, I am the one making karma.

154
00:12:03,755 --> 00:12:04,115
[SPEAKER_02]: But in

155
00:12:08,737 --> 00:12:13,659
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the mistake he's making, the karma, which is why Pratyūjana remains bound to saṃsāra.

156
00:12:14,660 --> 00:12:16,041
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a real thing.

157
00:12:17,141 --> 00:12:19,222
[SPEAKER_01]: If he finds out that it's a mistake, it ceases.

158
00:12:19,743 --> 00:12:20,423
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he's free.

159
00:12:22,064 --> 00:12:25,986
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, it's not just an illusion or something like that, because saṃsāra is real.

160
00:12:26,426 --> 00:12:28,367
[SPEAKER_01]: And results of the saṃsāra are very real.

161
00:12:29,147 --> 00:12:31,329
[SPEAKER_01]: And results of one's actions are very, very real.

162
00:12:32,169 --> 00:12:33,850
[SPEAKER_01]: Like you read in the Sutta, sometimes people did

163
00:12:34,632 --> 00:12:38,514
[SPEAKER_01]: A little thing that was wrong, but then it resulted in a lot later on.

164
00:12:39,555 --> 00:12:45,038
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's never a guarantee, thinking, oh, I'll be fine or not, which means that responsibility is real.

165
00:12:46,719 --> 00:12:53,622
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for as long as the person doesn't admit the mistake, the misunderstanding, misappropriation, he won't be able to fix it.

166
00:12:56,044 --> 00:13:00,967
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sometimes people are too keen to fix it, so they quickly fall into denial of it.

167
00:13:02,163 --> 00:13:03,904
[SPEAKER_01]: Not self, not self, not mine, not self.

168
00:13:04,124 --> 00:13:06,505
[SPEAKER_01]: The science has proved nothing is self, nothing is mine.

169
00:13:07,126 --> 00:13:10,027
[SPEAKER_01]: But that just obscures, that doesn't accept the responsibility.

170
00:13:11,828 --> 00:13:20,073
[SPEAKER_01]: If you start saying to yourself, not self, not mine, through the acceptance of that responsibility and never abandoning that responsibility, that's a different thing.

171
00:13:20,713 --> 00:13:27,277
[SPEAKER_01]: But usually people do that, they say to themselves, not self, in order to avoid the responsibility.

172
00:13:28,192 --> 00:13:30,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it feels like a fix, but it's not.

173
00:13:30,774 --> 00:13:35,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's a temporary fix, which means it doesn't really work in the long term.

174
00:13:41,301 --> 00:13:46,244
[SPEAKER_02]: How do I... disowning the... accepting it is... it is...

175
00:13:46,405 --> 00:13:47,545
[SPEAKER_02]: I am the one...

176
00:13:47,685 --> 00:13:48,986
[SPEAKER_02]: I am responsible for it.

177
00:13:49,927 --> 00:13:50,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, disowning...

178
00:13:50,928 --> 00:13:54,210
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, I use that word, but it's not... it's not like you don't do it.

179
