[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not the same.

363
00:27:17,873 --> 00:27:19,173
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't come from the world.

364
00:27:19,253 --> 00:27:21,894
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't say, oh, they're all in the same place and they react differently.

365
00:27:21,954 --> 00:27:22,474
[SPEAKER_01]: No, they're not.

366
00:27:24,405 --> 00:27:37,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Their places correspond by having in common that they're all in the monastery, but the internal aspect of it, me, my mood, my mind, my feelings, that's unique to all the aggregates individually, which is why there's difference between individuals.

367
00:27:38,968 --> 00:27:40,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Even in terms of arahants.

368
00:27:40,909 --> 00:27:48,674
[SPEAKER_01]: So, so you can't separate yourself and think that the place is the situation and then try to explain it to yourself back.

369
00:27:48,694 --> 00:27:49,915
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, you are already there.

370
00:27:50,075 --> 00:27:51,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Already connected.

371
00:27:51,436 --> 00:27:52,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you're part of it.

372
00:27:52,917 --> 00:27:54,438
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... you're inseparable from it.

373
00:27:54,738 --> 00:28:01,442
[SPEAKER_01]: It just... it wouldn't make any... it would be inconceivable to think you're apart from it, unless it's based on a mistake.

374
00:28:05,064 --> 00:28:07,586
[SPEAKER_02]: It means that that mood... that is... that is a general...

375
00:28:08,436 --> 00:28:08,697
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes.

376
00:28:10,478 --> 00:28:14,081
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mood is, you know, it's not... like, you know, I can be...

377
00:28:14,142 --> 00:28:21,428
[SPEAKER_01]: I can be in a bad mood, but particularly I can be doing good things, if I... if I don't allow myself to be moved by my bad mood.

378
00:28:22,849 --> 00:28:26,793
[SPEAKER_01]: But generally, the bad mood is there, and it's kind of directing me.

379
00:28:26,813 --> 00:28:30,636
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I might be doing good things because I want to counter my bad mood.

380
00:28:31,137 --> 00:28:33,499
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still... that's prompted by my bad mood.

381
00:28:34,301 --> 00:28:38,763
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that general slope is there, and then I'm kind of going down it and choosing things.

382
00:28:38,783 --> 00:28:41,144
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can always choose wholesome on account of unwholesome.

383
00:28:41,964 --> 00:28:46,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're in the worst possible mood, there is never a justification for unwholesome.

384
00:28:48,807 --> 00:28:54,149
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, when the mood changes, your particular thing might change, or might not, if you have enough wisdom.

385
00:28:55,090 --> 00:29:02,073
[SPEAKER_01]: If you develop enough wisdom to keep choosing wholesome over unwholesome, keep attending wholesome thoughts over unwholesome, despite how you feel,

386
00:29:03,056 --> 00:29:10,661
[SPEAKER_01]: How much pull you experience, how much bad bad influence you get from the world or whatever, it's never...

387
00:29:10,701 --> 00:29:11,002
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all...

388
00:29:11,022 --> 00:29:13,463
[SPEAKER_01]: If you choose to do bad, it's because you've chosen to do bad.

389
00:29:13,904 --> 00:29:16,465
[SPEAKER_01]: You've chosen that to be the reason for your bad action.

390
00:29:17,206 --> 00:29:19,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you've chosen that to be a good reason for your good action.

391
00:29:20,948 --> 00:29:22,009
[SPEAKER_01]: You're always responsible.

392
00:29:22,790 --> 00:29:24,691
[SPEAKER_01]: So, no matter how bad the mood is, you...

393
00:29:25,236 --> 00:29:26,297
[SPEAKER_01]: You are always responsible.

394
00:29:26,317 --> 00:29:28,298
[SPEAKER_01]: But it is important to start seeing it first.

395
00:29:28,818 --> 00:29:37,524
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you don't see the general background of your mood, of the direction your mind is in, you will not see a responsibility in regard to that.

396
00:29:38,265 --> 00:29:39,085
[SPEAKER_01]: You've just been moved.

397
00:29:39,105 --> 00:29:45,150
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have people who, when they're in a bad mood, they say things to people, they say out loud, they offend people, they hurt people.

398
00:29:45,870 --> 00:29:47,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Then they feel bad afterwards.

399
00:29:47,291 --> 00:29:50,553
[SPEAKER_01]: When they kind of come out of the bad mood, they apologize.

400
00:29:50,633 --> 00:29:51,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I'll never do that again.

401
00:29:51,574 --> 00:29:52,354
[SPEAKER_01]: Then they do it again.

402
00:29:53,330 --> 00:29:57,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you have people, when they're in a good mood, they're so excited, ecstatic, they get a bit too much.

403
00:29:58,835 --> 00:30:01,978
[SPEAKER_01]: Then afterwards, they feel a bit, oh God, I got a bit carried away.

404
00:30:02,839 --> 00:30:06,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they're not aware of the general situation, of the general direction of their mood.

405
00:30:07,443 --> 00:30:12,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, by not being aware, they don't have a safeguard, so they're always taken by it.

406
00:30:15,210 --> 00:30:16,491
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the mindfulness is.

407
00:30:17,815 --> 00:30:25,839
[SPEAKER_02]: Even if before you experienced that mountain and that slope and that's already included means that mood, that itself was a mood.

408
00:30:25,859 --> 00:30:26,359
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

409
00:30:26,399 --> 00:30:30,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Not something like a... No, not the actual location.

410
00:30:31,001 --> 00:30:32,062
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

411
00:30:32,702 --> 00:30:37,464
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that is the reason that some people, even when they stay in the temple, their mood is not not good.

412
00:30:37,584 --> 00:30:38,125
[SPEAKER_02]: Exactly.

413
00:30:38,485 --> 00:30:39,185
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the reason.

414
00:30:39,405 --> 00:30:43,187
[SPEAKER_01]: They can be sitting right next to the Buddha and still... Yeah.

415
00:30:43,347 --> 00:30:45,328
[SPEAKER_01]: There were cases like, like they, you know,

416
00:30:46,067 --> 00:30:47,988
[SPEAKER_01]: In the assembly of the Buddha, but they just would not.

417
00:30:48,008 --> 00:30:49,089
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind wouldn't be there.

418
00:30:50,610 --> 00:30:52,251
[SPEAKER_01]: And they say, well, you're wasting the opportunity.

419
00:30:53,091 --> 00:30:55,573
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, it doesn't necessarily mean much.

420
00:30:55,873 --> 00:31:02,617
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, going to a place of the temple can remind you, oh, well, it can remind you if you were to know what you're supposed to do.

421
00:31:03,697 --> 00:31:09,281
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you were to know that you're supposed to be mindful of your moods and take responsibility for your choices,

422
00:31:10,283 --> 00:31:15,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, going to a monastery or reading the suttas can remind you of that when you forget.

423
00:31:15,544 --> 00:31:23,186
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't know that in the first place, well, no amount of visits to the monastery will make a difference, because you can't be reminded of something that you don't know.

424
00:31:24,566 --> 00:31:28,507
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you'll probably get different things then in the monastery, and people will go for different reasons.

425
00:31:29,127 --> 00:31:31,468
[SPEAKER_01]: For a monastery, superstition or whatever.

426
00:31:32,128 --> 00:31:33,148
[SPEAKER_01]: Usually, it's a superstition.

427
00:31:33,648 --> 00:31:40,150
[SPEAKER_01]: But regardless of the religion as well, whether it's Buddhism or Christianity, it's all usually... That's the reason why people go to churches and so on.

428
00:31:42,105 --> 00:31:47,927
[SPEAKER_02]: It's quite hard to maintain mindfulness with thinking about the background.

429
00:31:48,087 --> 00:31:51,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because you're trying to maintain mindfulness in a particular sense.

430
00:31:51,148 --> 00:31:52,509
[SPEAKER_01]: You're trying to keep it in front of you.

431
00:31:53,029 --> 00:31:54,189
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not mindfulness.

432
00:31:55,330 --> 00:31:59,011
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness is knowing the background without interfering with it.

433
00:31:59,671 --> 00:32:02,112
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if I ask you now, are you in a good or a bad mood?

434
00:32:02,212 --> 00:32:03,233
[SPEAKER_01]: You will know the answer.

435
00:32:04,193 --> 00:32:08,454
[SPEAKER_01]: You might not be able to define it specifically, but you will know, it's all right.

436
00:32:08,594 --> 00:32:10,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, no, I'm not too good.

437
00:32:10,760 --> 00:32:12,682
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's... that knowledge is already there.

438
00:32:12,822 --> 00:32:15,304
[SPEAKER_02]: It means no need to see the place where I'm in.

439
00:32:15,324 --> 00:32:15,785
[SPEAKER_02]: Not that.

440
00:32:15,805 --> 00:32:17,686
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the mood that I have to see.

441
00:32:17,706 --> 00:32:24,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the fact that you can give me the answer to where your mood is, at any time, means that knowledge is there at any time.

442
00:32:24,172 --> 00:32:25,794
[SPEAKER_01]: Already the situation is included.

443
00:32:25,854 --> 00:32:26,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

444
00:32:26,174 --> 00:32:29,917
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's something which can be mind... Only that is necessary to be mindful of.

445
00:32:30,057 --> 00:32:30,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

446
00:32:31,992 --> 00:32:36,595
[SPEAKER_02]: Before, I remember you said that I'm sitting in the cave, kuti, and something, something.

447
00:32:36,736 --> 00:32:38,137
[SPEAKER_02]: You were explaining about situation.

448
00:32:38,197 --> 00:32:39,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it included in all those inclinations?

449
00:32:40,198 --> 00:32:46,603
[SPEAKER_01]: I am sitting, I am doing, I am means, yeah, means basically you are in such and such situation.

450
00:32:48,024 --> 00:32:49,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means your mood is there.

451
00:32:49,345 --> 00:32:52,467
[SPEAKER_02]: So, is it important to see that I am sitting in the kuti?

452
00:32:52,587 --> 00:32:53,368
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it important?

453
00:32:53,388 --> 00:32:56,850
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that was just the example I gave you, as a more general situation.

454
00:32:56,870 --> 00:32:59,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I am in the monastery now.

455
00:32:59,352 --> 00:33:00,553
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be mindful of that.

456
00:33:01,296 --> 00:33:06,962
[SPEAKER_01]: You can be mindful of, I am, I'm feeling elated now, or I'm feeling depressed now.

457
00:33:06,982 --> 00:33:13,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, like in satipaṭṭhāna, when the Buddha refers to the mind, he says, he knows mind, like, literally, for what it is.

458
00:33:13,868 --> 00:33:16,170
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows elated mind as elated mind.

459
00:33:16,310 --> 00:33:18,633
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows depressed mind as depressed mind.

460
00:33:19,073 --> 00:33:24,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Happy mind as happy, expanded mind, shrunk mind, like different shapes the mind can take.

461
00:33:24,558 --> 00:33:26,180
[SPEAKER_01]: He knows them, but that's it.

462
00:33:27,170 --> 00:33:28,812
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not trying to change it or whatever.

463
00:33:28,832 --> 00:33:34,137
[SPEAKER_01]: You just, ah, the mind is in that state, which means it will be affecting my particular choices.

464
00:33:34,237 --> 00:33:48,490
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if the mind is in depressed mood, you're mindful of it, you will be more aware of the choices you make, which means you will not be making bad choices, because you know that the mind will be trying to get you to make the bad choices, because the mind is depressed.

465
00:33:48,930 --> 00:33:50,452
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, when the mind is happy,

466
00:33:50,963 --> 00:33:57,685
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be overreaching in terms of your great choices, because you know, the mind is elated, which means the perspective is a bit lost.

467
00:33:58,745 --> 00:34:06,107
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to have the mind even, but, like, even an arahant will practice mindfulness in terms of the different shapes his mind will take.

468
00:34:06,787 --> 00:34:12,048
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that he completely, completely severed any bond with that mind, or with the body.

469
00:34:12,649 --> 00:34:15,469
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whichever shape his mind takes, it doesn't affect him.

470
00:34:16,510 --> 00:34:17,730
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, he's gone beyond.

471
00:34:18,548 --> 00:34:20,790
[SPEAKER_01]: But until that's the case, you need to keep that in mind.

472
00:34:20,810 --> 00:34:25,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're only aware or mindful of what's in front of you, it means you're not seeing your mind.

473
00:34:25,935 --> 00:34:27,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Because your mind is never in front of you.

474
00:34:28,117 --> 00:34:28,958
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always in the back.

475
00:34:29,779 --> 00:34:32,041
[SPEAKER_02]: I admit that seeing situation is in front of me.

476
00:34:32,521 --> 00:34:36,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you're seeing situation in front of you, that's not your situation anymore.

477
00:34:36,125 --> 00:34:38,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Situation is where the mind is, which is always in the background.

478
00:34:39,808 --> 00:34:42,711
[SPEAKER_01]: Mood is where the mind is, which is always in the background.

479
00:34:43,489 --> 00:34:48,410
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you try to see it in front of you, it means whatever you're seeing in front of you is not the background.

480
00:34:49,191 --> 00:34:53,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it was the background before, the fact that you put it in front means something else is now.

481
00:34:54,892 --> 00:35:00,994
[SPEAKER_02]: It means only us to keep seeing the... like a mood, like a mental state.

482
00:35:01,194 --> 00:35:04,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you have to keep seeing the relationship between the two.

483
00:35:04,435 --> 00:35:12,437
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to keep seeing the mood, the mental state, and the relationship between your particular situation, like particular choices you make.

484
00:35:13,431 --> 00:35:18,213
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, this intention... Means, things I'm about to do, based on how I feel.

485
00:35:19,054 --> 00:35:21,455
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, to put it simply, yeah.

486
00:35:22,876 --> 00:35:25,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I feel good, I feel bad, I feel neutral.

487
00:35:25,797 --> 00:35:26,897
[SPEAKER_01]: What am I doing right now?

488
00:35:27,358 --> 00:35:30,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whatever you do, actually, is rooted in how you feel.

489
00:35:30,559 --> 00:35:36,142
[SPEAKER_02]: Bodily feeling or, I don't know, suppose there's something that I feel each year that is... No, those are not feelings, those are all perceptions.

490
00:35:36,962 --> 00:35:38,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, each is a perception.

491
00:35:39,318 --> 00:35:42,080
[SPEAKER_01]: You might dislike... you might dislike your itch.

492
00:35:42,500 --> 00:35:53,246
[SPEAKER_01]: So, experience displeasure in your mind on account of the bodily itch, but it's not that you're experiencing displeasure in your body as a sensation.

493
00:35:53,266 --> 00:35:55,028
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's just a perception of touch.

494
00:35:55,768 --> 00:35:58,350
[SPEAKER_02]: Pain is feeling, isn't it?

495
00:35:58,390 --> 00:35:59,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Pain is feeling, yes.

496
00:35:59,690 --> 00:36:01,611
[SPEAKER_02]: Pain is perception.

497
00:36:01,652 --> 00:36:01,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

498
00:36:02,312 --> 00:36:09,036
[SPEAKER_01]: So, itch can be painful or sometimes itch can be pleasant, depending on whatever people are into.

499
00:36:09,315 --> 00:36:11,797
[SPEAKER_02]: Pain, I think you say that, the unprisoned feeling.

500
00:36:11,817 --> 00:36:14,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, pain is unprisoned.

501
00:36:15,099 --> 00:36:16,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Every feeling is mental.

502
00:36:17,081 --> 00:36:18,062
[SPEAKER_01]: And people misunderstand.

503
00:36:18,102 --> 00:36:20,904
[SPEAKER_01]: They think, because when Buddha said, ah, there are bodily feelings.

504
00:36:20,964 --> 00:36:23,906
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he said the feelings caused in regard to the body.

505
00:36:24,707 --> 00:36:28,229
[SPEAKER_01]: So people misunderstand that and use that to explain sensation.

506
00:36:28,309 --> 00:36:29,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, that's my bodily feeling.

507
00:36:29,730 --> 00:36:30,271
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's not.

508
00:36:31,111 --> 00:36:35,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Every feeling is cetāsika, is the Pali word, means belonging to... it's mental.

509
00:36:37,073 --> 00:36:37,514
[SPEAKER_01]: Mental.

510
00:36:37,814 --> 00:36:41,696
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if it's in regard to the body, then it's called bodily feeling.

511
00:36:42,116 --> 00:36:43,416
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still a mental feeling.

512
00:36:44,157 --> 00:36:47,498
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's in regard to the mind, then it's kind of doubly mental feeling.

513
00:36:47,598 --> 00:36:51,520
[SPEAKER_01]: But either feeling being in regard to body or mind, it's mental in itself.

514
00:36:52,841 --> 00:37:01,085
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I said, when you have a bodily itch, bodily discomfort, you will most likely experience mentally unpleasant feeling.

515
00:37:01,205 --> 00:37:01,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

516
00:37:02,171 --> 00:37:04,752
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not that unpleasant feeling is in your leg.

517
00:37:05,472 --> 00:37:11,953
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a very sharp sensation of severed leg and a kind of almost almost unbearable pain mentally.

518
00:37:13,294 --> 00:37:17,935
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're mentally free from that pain, that discomfort ceases to cause you suffering.

519
00:37:20,555 --> 00:37:22,316
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why that's why like the...

520
00:37:25,188 --> 00:37:31,013
[SPEAKER_01]: The arahants who have their minds developed in terms of jhānas, they could bear a lot of physical discomfort.

521
00:37:31,433 --> 00:37:32,974
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't be discomfort for them.

522
00:37:33,655 --> 00:37:36,717
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the way the mind relates to it.

523
00:37:36,958 --> 00:37:43,463
[SPEAKER_01]: When the Buddha asked Mahākassapa, said, ah, well, you're very old now, you've done what there's to be done, why don't you come with me?

524
00:37:44,564 --> 00:37:53,171
[SPEAKER_01]: I get lots of offerings, I get silk robes, stay in these palaces, so you can come and stay with me, because there's no need for you to be living rough in the forest.

525
00:37:53,960 --> 00:38:09,748
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, oh, there's no need, Lord, because whoever can obtain jhānas and will, like these rag robes and these forests, will feel like the, you know, the softest, highest, most pleasant dwellings and abidings, because that's how it feels to the mind, that is strength in jhānas.

526
00:38:10,808 --> 00:38:19,993
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, another occasion, when the Buddha was instructing a very well-known ruler, when he said, develop jhānas so that the head robes you're wearing will feel like silk.

527
00:38:20,974 --> 00:38:28,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Developed jhāna so that the sleeping under the root of tree will feel like sleeping in a plastered palace.

528
00:38:28,740 --> 00:38:39,368
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, oh, developed jhāna so that the rough, unclean rice you eat would feel like the softest rice with the nicest curries.

529
00:38:41,106 --> 00:38:43,028
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, it was for medicine and so on.

530
00:38:43,428 --> 00:38:49,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Saying, basically, that the mind state, the mind development will determine your relationship with your body.

531
00:38:50,153 --> 00:38:51,795
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the body is going through discomfort.

532
00:38:52,295 --> 00:38:58,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, whether you're gonna suffer... Well, whether it... Usually, discomfort is associated with pain, mental pain.

533
00:38:59,098 --> 00:39:00,779
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if there is no discomfort, there is no pain.

534
00:39:01,360 --> 00:39:02,961
[SPEAKER_02]: Associated with mental pain.

535
00:39:03,101 --> 00:39:03,601
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

536
00:39:03,621 --> 00:39:05,783
[SPEAKER_01]: Experiencing suffering in regard to discomfort.

537
00:39:06,063 --> 00:39:08,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody dislikes discomfort.

538
00:39:08,845 --> 00:39:13,328
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you develop your mind, means less and less thing will be discomfortable to you.

539
00:39:14,108 --> 00:39:14,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Uncomfortable.

540
00:39:14,809 --> 00:39:16,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Uncomfortable.

541
00:39:17,150 --> 00:39:17,310
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

542
00:39:17,330 --> 00:39:19,292
[SPEAKER_02]: Arahants, they have unpleasant feelings.

543
00:39:19,312 --> 00:39:19,692
[SPEAKER_01]: They do.

544
00:39:19,752 --> 00:39:20,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

545
00:39:20,172 --> 00:39:20,713
[SPEAKER_01]: They can still...

546
00:39:20,733 --> 00:39:20,953
[SPEAKER_01]: But...

547
00:39:21,253 --> 00:39:24,875
[SPEAKER_01]: So, arahant can be an arahant without being mastered all of the jhānas.

548
00:39:25,576 --> 00:39:28,678
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you had arahants who were in great pain physically.

549
00:39:29,398 --> 00:39:35,402
[SPEAKER_01]: Experiencing sharp, piercing sensations, piercing perceptions on account of which...

550
00:39:35,422 --> 00:39:36,883
[SPEAKER_01]: But they were not suffering internally.

551
00:39:37,123 --> 00:39:37,904
[SPEAKER_01]: There was no dukkha.

552
00:39:38,584 --> 00:39:44,027
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's just that their relationship with the body was kind of unbearable and pointless, because they were arahants.

553
00:39:44,608 --> 00:39:48,630
[SPEAKER_01]: They had no reason to endure all of those sharp discomforts.

554
00:39:49,091 --> 00:39:51,772
[SPEAKER_01]: But discomfort in itself is not actually painful.

555
00:39:53,512 --> 00:40:04,420
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the the mental resistance in regard to that makes you suffer on account of discomfort, makes you fear discomfort, makes you really work hard to not experience discomfort.

556
00:40:05,121 --> 00:40:05,581
[SPEAKER_02]: Wait a minute.

557
00:40:05,701 --> 00:40:09,584
[SPEAKER_02]: Anahant, they feel unpleasant feelings, but not suffering.

558
00:40:10,605 --> 00:40:18,450
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, relatively speaking, like, you can't say that, because if you say for Pūtūjana that he feels unpleasant feelings, you can't use the same designation for an anahant.

559
00:40:19,889 --> 00:40:22,851
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, from a spiritual point of view, Arahant doesn't have any feelings anymore.

560
00:40:22,971 --> 00:40:23,751
[SPEAKER_01]: He has overcome them.

561
00:40:24,231 --> 00:40:27,453
[SPEAKER_01]: And the fact that he doesn't feel anything anymore, that's what's pleasant for him.

562
00:40:29,634 --> 00:40:31,755
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what Sariputta said.

563
00:40:32,316 --> 00:40:44,482
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to distinguish the bodily discomfort from pain, from displeasure, from dukkha.

564
00:40:44,642 --> 00:40:47,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Because being able to bear discomfort,

565
00:40:48,141 --> 00:40:49,602
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sometimes it's necessary.

566
00:40:49,642 --> 00:40:52,123
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why for some people asceticism would work.

567
00:40:52,923 --> 00:40:54,644
[SPEAKER_01]: It will make them see things rightly.

568
00:40:55,184 --> 00:40:57,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Not for everyone, but for some people it would.

569
00:40:58,105 --> 00:41:05,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why the Buddha said, well, if your greed, aversion and delusion decreases while doing ascetic practices, then do it.

570
00:41:05,869 --> 00:41:07,349
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's not decreasing, then don't do it.

571
00:41:08,090 --> 00:41:12,832
[SPEAKER_01]: But yes, some people do need to go through discomfort in order to start to see the difference.

572
00:41:13,690 --> 00:41:15,751
[SPEAKER_01]: The discomfort is not automatically a pain.

573
00:41:16,392 --> 00:41:20,134
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your mental resistance in regard to it, that makes you suffer, makes you confused.

574
00:41:21,635 --> 00:41:25,097
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by overcoming that, you overcome discomfort, and then you don't fear it.

575
00:41:25,677 --> 00:41:31,520
[SPEAKER_01]: And as the Buddha did, he... well, he was the Buddha, so he completely overcome... he didn't have to pursue discomfort anymore.

576
00:41:31,620 --> 00:41:32,061
[SPEAKER_01]: What for?

577
00:41:33,161 --> 00:41:36,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, he was not affected by it, so he would just go wherever he was.

578
00:41:36,823 --> 00:41:39,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes he would sleep on a stack of hay, under the tree.

579
00:41:39,645 --> 00:41:41,306
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes he would sleep in a king's palace.

580
00:41:41,819 --> 00:41:46,021
[SPEAKER_01]: If that's where he was at the time, got invited or whatever else.

581
00:41:46,181 --> 00:41:52,983
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not... it's not the external things will determine, necessarily, whether it's good or bad.

582
00:41:53,023 --> 00:41:54,784
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically your attitude in regard to it.

583
00:41:55,784 --> 00:42:02,407
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, sometimes people can go and live roughly in the forest and under the root of a tree for completely wrong reasons.

584
00:42:03,567 --> 00:42:04,728
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't see this difference.

585
00:42:04,768 --> 00:42:10,570
[SPEAKER_01]: They're just torturing themselves and thinking that through torture, they will be developing purification and strength.

586
00:42:11,217 --> 00:42:11,697
[SPEAKER_01]: But they won't.

587
00:42:12,097 --> 00:42:16,118
[SPEAKER_01]: They'll develop endurance, but misguided endurance.

588
00:42:16,138 --> 00:42:18,199
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the endurance has to be rooted in right view.

589
00:42:18,319 --> 00:42:20,759
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, anything has to be rooted in right view in order to be right.

590
00:42:21,740 --> 00:42:31,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why a person shouldn't go to a forest and start exerting himself unless it's a sotāpanna, unless he has jhānas, unless other establishments have been made.

591
00:42:32,266 --> 00:42:32,826
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha said.

592
00:42:32,846 --> 00:42:35,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Because otherwise, you just end up doing it for unwholesome reasons.

593
00:42:35,568 --> 00:42:38,370
[SPEAKER_02]: And in your answer, it's actually a very difficult point to understand.

594
00:42:38,390 --> 00:42:44,933
[SPEAKER_02]: Because sometimes I feel that because of the self-view, that they try to go against, like, try to change the things.

595
00:42:45,614 --> 00:42:46,114
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

596
00:42:46,174 --> 00:42:49,436
[SPEAKER_02]: But that is actually based on the self-view.

597
00:42:49,676 --> 00:42:54,599
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, like, again, even if it is based on the self-view, it's real as such.

598
00:42:55,659 --> 00:43:00,102
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have a thought of wanting to change things, that's something you include in your mindfulness.

599
00:43:01,263 --> 00:43:05,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it came from yourself, from the mistake of yourself or not, it's real as such.

600
00:43:06,007 --> 00:43:06,387
[SPEAKER_01]: It's there.

601
00:43:06,487 --> 00:43:07,508
[SPEAKER_01]: You just experienced it.

602
00:43:08,369 --> 00:43:09,330
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're mindful of that.

603
00:43:11,031 --> 00:43:11,772
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't divide.

604
00:43:11,812 --> 00:43:12,672
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this came from this.

605
00:43:12,692 --> 00:43:13,173
[SPEAKER_01]: That's okay.

606
00:43:13,193 --> 00:43:13,673
[SPEAKER_01]: This came from...

607
00:43:13,693 --> 00:43:14,494
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want to... No.

608
00:43:14,634 --> 00:43:17,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you experience, it's real as that experience.

609
00:43:19,098 --> 00:43:26,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's an abstract experience of something that you've never seen, taste, touch, you experienced it in your mind as such, and it's real in that way.

610
00:43:28,025 --> 00:43:29,306
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're having

611
00:43:30,097 --> 00:43:33,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Resistance in regard to endurance, that's part of the endurance.

612
00:43:34,799 --> 00:43:38,301
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have thoughts of resisting this, that's all result of you enduring.

613
00:43:38,601 --> 00:43:42,042
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just include it in endurance.

614
00:43:42,222 --> 00:43:45,584
[SPEAKER_01]: Arahant can endure.

615
00:43:45,604 --> 00:43:49,085
[SPEAKER_01]: But Arahant won't suffer on account of his endurance.

616
00:43:49,126 --> 00:43:50,566
[SPEAKER_01]: He won't fear the endurance.

617
00:43:52,147 --> 00:43:53,207
[SPEAKER_01]: He won't try to avoid it.

618
00:43:55,537 --> 00:43:56,457
[SPEAKER_01]: If it comes, it comes.

619
00:43:56,517 --> 00:43:57,898
[SPEAKER_01]: If it doesn't, it doesn't.

620
00:43:58,358 --> 00:44:00,858
[SPEAKER_01]: He won't try to avoid it, but he won't go after it either.

621
00:44:02,299 --> 00:44:05,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Because he has no need for it.

622
00:44:05,520 --> 00:44:12,361
[SPEAKER_02]: Again, it needs to be done for the wholesome reasons.

623
00:44:12,762 --> 00:44:15,682
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, patujana can endure a lot.

624
00:44:15,722 --> 00:44:21,484
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, there are people who set themselves on fire, complete misguided patujanas, for ridiculous political reasons.

625
00:44:22,642 --> 00:44:24,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, how dumb is that?

626
00:44:24,722 --> 00:44:25,843
[SPEAKER_01]: Completely misguided.

627
00:44:26,163 --> 00:44:28,543
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, it's extreme for endurance and people do it.

628
00:44:30,364 --> 00:44:31,284
[SPEAKER_01]: And that means nothing.

629
00:44:32,264 --> 00:44:36,965
[SPEAKER_01]: But if somebody would endure very little for the right reasons, he would reap much more benefit.

630
00:44:38,405 --> 00:44:50,048
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you endure because the endurance has arisen, because you don't go after it, nor do you run away from it, nor do you do anything on account of it, that's already more wholesome.

631
00:44:51,529 --> 00:44:58,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can know endurance for what it is, or lack of it for what it is, or need of development for what it is.

632
00:44:59,433 --> 00:45:06,316
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be running away from it, because sometimes people get really into endurance as a form of running away from it.

633
00:45:07,057 --> 00:45:10,218
[SPEAKER_01]: I fear this, so I'm going to go and face it, because I'm terrified of it.

634
00:45:10,739 --> 00:45:15,961
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, they end up torturing themselves, as if that will kind of make them overcome the fear.

635
00:45:16,882 --> 00:45:19,603
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is any wisdom in regard to it, it might help them.

636
00:45:20,051 --> 00:45:24,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, putting themselves in a rough situation might help them, if there is a wisdom basis.

637
00:45:25,033 --> 00:45:28,074
[SPEAKER_01]: But if there isn't, it will just make them revolve around the same issue.

638
00:45:30,194 --> 00:45:46,140
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes, actually, even when... even when we don't do anything, automatically, body starts moving, and sometimes it's quite interesting, sometimes just sitting, and not thinking to do anything, but ultimately the body starts moving, it starts doing, it starts thinking.

639
00:45:46,160 --> 00:45:47,380
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, it carries on.

640
00:45:47,480 --> 00:45:49,381
[SPEAKER_02]: But really, there is no suffering, but eh...

641
00:45:55,248 --> 00:46:00,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's basically, as I said, the body doesn't need self to operate.

642
00:46:01,829 --> 00:46:04,530
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentions don't need self to operate.

643
00:46:05,030 --> 00:46:06,550
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they've been operating on their own.

644
00:46:07,550 --> 00:46:11,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Before, for putusana, or after, for anāgāmī.

645
00:46:12,011 --> 00:46:17,192
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is, putusana first needs to accept the responsibility for himself,

646
00:46:18,320 --> 00:46:21,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is done through developing mindfulness in regard to body and whatever else.

647
00:46:21,723 --> 00:46:23,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Actions, choices, specifically.

648
00:46:24,965 --> 00:46:33,292
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, through accepting the responsibility, you can eventually reach a point when you just see, oh, yeah, the intentionality doesn't require sense of self.

649
00:46:33,552 --> 00:46:34,313
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all operating.

650
00:46:34,894 --> 00:46:39,878
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you start doing it before you fully accept responsibility, then it will more be like more forgetfulness.

651
00:46:40,838 --> 00:46:43,420
[SPEAKER_01]: You forgot to be mindful and, yeah, the body is just doing things.

652
00:46:43,901 --> 00:46:47,123
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's all rooted in our wholesome, because of the self-view.

653
00:46:48,293 --> 00:47:03,399
[SPEAKER_01]: So, Arahant's forgetfulness, so to speak, is different from the forget... like, Arahant's forgetfulness will not induce self-view, will not induce a vijaya, which means, basically, it's not really forgetfulness.

654
00:47:04,740 --> 00:47:07,661
[SPEAKER_01]: But for Kuntujan, I mean, he is forgetful, means he forgets all the way.

655
00:47:09,742 --> 00:47:13,263
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how the vijaya ignorance is maintained.

656
00:47:17,571 --> 00:47:25,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, so to speak, but the thing is, like, if your forgetfulness is not inducing a vision, it means you're mindful of you being forgetful.

657
00:47:26,420 --> 00:47:29,242
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might be forgetful in terms of particular things.

658
00:47:29,322 --> 00:47:32,726
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I forgot the number or something, but I didn't forget the nature of forgetting.

659
00:47:33,066 --> 00:47:33,907
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm mindful of that.

660
00:47:35,132 --> 00:47:37,654
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Arahant's mindfulness is sort of fully developed.

661
00:47:38,014 --> 00:47:40,135
[SPEAKER_01]: And he can't stop doing it, so to speak.

662
00:47:40,155 --> 00:47:41,456
[SPEAKER_01]: He's always mindful in that sense.

663
00:47:41,476 --> 00:47:46,560
[SPEAKER_01]: But he's not always mindful in a sense of contemporary vipassana, like touching, moving, like a robot.

664
00:47:46,580 --> 00:47:46,720
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

665
00:47:47,580 --> 00:47:50,282
[SPEAKER_01]: It means he can't forget his forgetfulness.

666
00:47:51,583 --> 00:47:54,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Forgetfulness will not induce avijja anymore for him.

667
00:47:56,126 --> 00:48:01,750
[SPEAKER_01]: So even when he forgets particular things, as an experience of forgetting, he's mindful of that.

668
00:48:02,691 --> 00:48:04,772
[SPEAKER_01]: And that same cannot be said for patrujya.

669
00:48:06,704 --> 00:48:10,806
[SPEAKER_02]: Okay, sometimes you even think, I'm not mindful, actually, everything is going well.

670
00:48:10,846 --> 00:48:14,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, whether you're mindful or not, things are intentional and they're going.

671
00:48:15,348 --> 00:48:18,089
[SPEAKER_01]: The thing is, you do have to accept responsibility for it.

672
00:48:19,050 --> 00:48:24,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that's why the... that's why the situation of putusana is kind of perilous.

673
00:48:24,833 --> 00:48:31,036
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, you're not in control of everything, of anything, but in the same way, you're responsible for everything.

674
00:48:31,956 --> 00:48:35,118
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't control anything, but you have full responsibility for all of it.

675
00:48:36,481 --> 00:48:37,061
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's true.

676
00:48:37,682 --> 00:48:41,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Because patujanā, he might say, oh, well, I'm not doing this and it's just my body.

677
00:48:42,125 --> 00:48:46,888
[SPEAKER_01]: The fact is, when he dies, he'll be reborn again, because he's patujanā, and because of the actions he made.

678
00:48:46,948 --> 00:48:52,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if my body just made these actions, you will be experiencing those results, because you took that body to be yours.

679
00:48:54,434 --> 00:49:01,339
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, he needs to fully accept that responsibility, even for accidents, even for things that are not in his control.

680
00:49:01,399 --> 00:49:01,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Clear?

681
00:49:02,892 --> 00:49:05,933
[SPEAKER_01]: And through fully accepting the responsibility, he can eventually overcome it.

682
00:49:06,313 --> 00:49:07,014
[SPEAKER_01]: But not before.

683
00:49:08,314 --> 00:49:11,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, that's where the mundane right view comes first.

684
00:49:12,196 --> 00:49:12,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Like admitting.

685
00:49:14,376 --> 00:49:16,017
[SPEAKER_02]: Hmm.

686
00:49:16,237 --> 00:49:18,218
[SPEAKER_02]: Ārya is free from karma?

687
00:49:18,478 --> 00:49:20,058
[SPEAKER_02]: Arahant is free from karma, right?

688
00:49:20,078 --> 00:49:21,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, Ārya has a limited left over.

689
00:49:22,019 --> 00:49:22,659
[SPEAKER_02]: Really, very good.

690
00:49:22,759 --> 00:49:24,640
[SPEAKER_02]: Sotāpanna, is he free from karma?

691
00:49:24,740 --> 00:49:26,001
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, relatively.

692
00:49:26,101 --> 00:49:26,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Ultimately, yes.

693
00:49:26,921 --> 00:49:28,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Within seven, at most.

694
00:49:29,382 --> 00:49:30,302
[SPEAKER_01]: But he'll be done.

695
00:49:30,362 --> 00:49:31,863
[SPEAKER_01]: But he still has

696
00:49:33,193 --> 00:49:35,175
[SPEAKER_01]: The wheel didn't quite stop spinning yet.

697
00:49:35,555 --> 00:49:37,716
[SPEAKER_01]: But it will.

698
00:49:37,796 --> 00:49:39,697
[SPEAKER_02]: Without self-view, there's calm.

699
00:49:40,698 --> 00:49:41,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry?

700
00:49:41,258 --> 00:49:42,379
[SPEAKER_02]: Without self-view.

701
00:49:42,459 --> 00:49:45,821
[SPEAKER_02]: They have no self-view.

702
00:49:45,921 --> 00:49:47,242
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't believe in self.

703
00:49:47,562 --> 00:49:49,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but they still have residues of conceit.

704
00:49:50,484 --> 00:49:53,846
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't have a self-view in terms of thinking of themselves as living

705
00:49:54,425 --> 00:49:57,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Like an independent master of five aggregates.

706
00:49:57,406 --> 00:49:58,447
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have that anymore.

707
00:49:59,067 --> 00:50:04,850
[SPEAKER_01]: But they still have conceit, sense of I am, and they still have degree of greed, aversion, delusion.

708
00:50:05,050 --> 00:50:10,853
[SPEAKER_01]: Because of which, they will be revolving a bit longer, until they make enough effort, whether in this life or...

709
00:50:11,233 --> 00:50:14,575
[SPEAKER_01]: But Sarvabhaṇa doesn't necessarily have to be born seven times.

710
00:50:15,255 --> 00:50:19,477
[SPEAKER_01]: He can become another in his second lifetime, or third, or fourth, or fifth.

711
00:50:19,997 --> 00:50:21,058
[SPEAKER_01]: At most, seven.

712
00:50:24,401 --> 00:50:27,063
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, it's very difficult to understand the perception.

713
00:50:27,323 --> 00:50:30,305
[SPEAKER_02]: Perception is very difficult to do.

714
00:50:30,425 --> 00:50:35,328
[SPEAKER_02]: Perception means a lot like helping, knowledge to arise, no?

715
00:50:35,388 --> 00:50:36,409
[SPEAKER_02]: I think something like that.

716
00:50:36,629 --> 00:50:41,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, perception means perceiving, means things that arise, means things that you...

717
00:50:41,372 --> 00:50:43,714
[SPEAKER_02]: In the case of pain, you said pain is feeling.

718
00:50:43,754 --> 00:50:44,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

719
00:50:44,314 --> 00:50:48,137
[SPEAKER_02]: And perception means you said very like I am... Well, what is causing the pain?

720
00:50:51,324 --> 00:50:52,985
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the cause of pain.

721
00:50:53,105 --> 00:50:57,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not necessarily the cause of pain, but basically, perception and feeling are inseparable.

722
00:50:58,088 --> 00:50:59,788
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't just perceive or just feel.

723
00:50:59,949 --> 00:51:02,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever you feel, you're perceiving something.

724
00:51:03,430 --> 00:51:05,351
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever you're perceiving something, you're feeling something.

725
00:51:07,212 --> 00:51:11,355
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you say, I have a feeling of pain, well, there is a perception in regard to it.

726
00:51:12,175 --> 00:51:13,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Usually, it's what caused your pain.

727
00:51:15,617 --> 00:51:15,877
[SPEAKER_02]: Hmm.

728
00:51:16,217 --> 00:51:17,618
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what if I testify you?

729
00:51:19,059 --> 00:51:24,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's a sharp, piercing perception that you don't want to endure.

730
00:51:25,702 --> 00:51:26,923
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not necessarily pain.

731
00:51:30,044 --> 00:51:43,590
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody tells you, if you touch this fire, I will, I don't know, save 500 dying children, and you go and touch the fire, and you'll be very happy that you've done it, because you know you save 500 lives.

732
00:51:45,071 --> 00:51:47,132
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you won't be suffering on account of touching the fire.

733
00:51:49,053 --> 00:51:50,294
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you want... if you're...

734
00:51:50,634 --> 00:52:00,740
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know, if you're a painter, living out of your painting, and then you burn your hand, and you can't paint, you can't earn anymore, your family is suffering, you'll be suffering a lot on account of touching the fire.

735
00:52:01,220 --> 00:52:01,540
[SPEAKER_02]: Fine.

736
00:52:01,741 --> 00:52:03,742
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same perception, touching the fire.

737
00:52:04,622 --> 00:52:06,904
[SPEAKER_02]: Ah, perception instead of touching the fire?

738
00:52:07,064 --> 00:52:07,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

739
00:52:08,264 --> 00:52:10,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's a bodily perception of touch.

740
00:52:11,726 --> 00:52:13,988
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh.

741
00:52:14,008 --> 00:52:17,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing... seeing something disagreeable.

742
00:52:17,370 --> 00:52:17,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

743
00:52:17,943 --> 00:52:25,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can see something disagreeable or agreeable, but you don't have to neither suffer nor be happy on account of it, but usually people are.

744
00:52:26,007 --> 00:52:31,509
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, I see something agreeable, I like it, I want more of it, you get very elated.

745
00:52:32,389 --> 00:52:35,671
[SPEAKER_01]: You see something disagreeable, well, I don't want to look at it, I don't like it.

746
00:52:37,752 --> 00:52:40,473
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you have no choice but to look at it, you'll start suffering.

747
00:52:41,409 --> 00:52:50,256
[SPEAKER_02]: I think that if I... if I call it pain, it becomes a bit different, because I... like a... like a bodily pain, but actually it's unpleasant.

748
00:52:51,157 --> 00:52:58,663
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I was telling you, when you told me that, oh, getting rid of the bodily pains or whatever, I said, well, that's... that's irrelevant, in a way.

749
00:52:59,784 --> 00:53:02,627
[SPEAKER_01]: That has nothing to do to the nature of suffering, to dukkha.

750
00:53:03,487 --> 00:53:05,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, oh, I have a pain in my right arm.

751
00:53:05,149 --> 00:53:06,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, first of all, it's not in your right arm.

752
00:53:07,391 --> 00:53:08,892
[SPEAKER_01]: It's discomfort in your right arm.

753
00:53:09,618 --> 00:53:11,379
[SPEAKER_01]: On account of which, your mind is bothered.

754
00:53:12,079 --> 00:53:17,941
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you think the pain is in your right arm, then fixing your right arm will feel like you fixed your pain.

755
00:53:18,581 --> 00:53:22,782
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you're actually trying to fix your dukkha at a completely wrong domain.

756
00:53:24,183 --> 00:53:28,304
[SPEAKER_01]: You're trying to... and lots of meditation that people teach is about that.

757
00:53:28,805 --> 00:53:33,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Or observing the sensations and feeling your body like this, and if it feels tight, you loosen.

758
00:53:33,646 --> 00:53:36,047
[SPEAKER_01]: It's utterly relevant in every possible way.

759
00:53:37,391 --> 00:53:40,434
[SPEAKER_02]: How do you see that, eh, what it means, the Goenka?

760
00:53:40,454 --> 00:53:41,414
[SPEAKER_02]: Goenka to make...

761
00:53:41,534 --> 00:53:42,615
[SPEAKER_01]: It could not be more...

762
00:53:42,695 --> 00:53:43,896
[SPEAKER_02]: I, I attended before.

763
00:53:44,117 --> 00:53:45,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it could not be more misguided.

764
00:53:46,438 --> 00:53:48,240
[SPEAKER_01]: It's completely misprojected.

765
00:53:48,260 --> 00:53:48,680
[SPEAKER_01]: I, many people...

766
00:53:48,740 --> 00:53:50,121
[SPEAKER_01]: It's exactly what I'm saying just now.

767
00:53:50,161 --> 00:53:55,626
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you, you, you look in a completely wrong place for a solution to the problem of suffering.

768
00:53:56,066 --> 00:53:58,828
[SPEAKER_02]: Many people believe Goenka is the arahant or whatever.

769
00:53:58,869 --> 00:54:00,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah.

770
00:54:00,049 --> 00:54:01,391
[SPEAKER_01]: People believe various things.

771
00:54:01,931 --> 00:54:06,735
[SPEAKER_01]: The reason why, why people feel it works is because most of the people that do it

772
00:54:08,110 --> 00:54:15,794
[SPEAKER_01]: Our lay people, with very busy lives, and then, you never really stop, you never really reflect, you don't really have any right views.

773
00:54:16,395 --> 00:54:25,520
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you go to a place of an intense retreat, controlling it, with every moment this kind of tech, tech, you do this, you do this, you do this, you watch, you watch... And it almost feels like a release.

774
00:54:26,600 --> 00:54:32,123
[SPEAKER_01]: But the release comes from the novelty, from this new kind of compressed thing, that then brings out the release.

775
00:54:32,283 --> 00:54:35,005
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's nothing to do with wisdom.

776
00:54:35,525 --> 00:54:37,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you develop wisdom in regard to it,

777
00:54:37,945 --> 00:54:39,906
[SPEAKER_01]: It will not be on account of Goenka's techniques.

778
00:54:41,426 --> 00:54:47,429
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be on account of you practicing understanding, trying to understand, trying to find out what understanding means.

779
00:54:48,009 --> 00:54:51,230
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you do, you realize you don't have to do Goenka.

780
00:54:51,670 --> 00:54:54,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not more than go out by yourself and start sweeping leaves.

781
00:54:55,472 --> 00:54:56,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Or carry bricks, if you want.

782
00:54:56,772 --> 00:55:03,815
[SPEAKER_02]: It is like the treading, treading, seeing, following, keep observing, and going... Yeah, it's all based on body.

783
00:55:04,575 --> 00:55:05,155
[SPEAKER_02]: Based on body.

784
00:55:05,175 --> 00:55:07,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you observe bodily sensations, which is

785
00:55:07,773 --> 00:55:09,214
[SPEAKER_01]: You ordered your contradiction in terms.

786
00:55:09,614 --> 00:55:12,296
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't feel your body, you perceive it.

787
00:55:14,097 --> 00:55:27,004
[SPEAKER_01]: You can experience feelings in your mind in regard to what body is going through, especially if you identify with your body of material form, you'll suffer even more then.

788
00:55:27,204 --> 00:55:32,287
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, any suffering that happens or not, happens on the level of mind, not on the body.

789
00:55:32,868 --> 00:55:35,129
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, removing a particular knee pain,

790
00:55:36,203 --> 00:55:39,826
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... it's plain ridiculous, when it comes to understanding the nature of suffering.

791
00:55:40,446 --> 00:55:42,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, why would this be relevant in any way?

792
00:55:42,388 --> 00:55:44,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, just stretch your knee if it hurts.

793
00:55:45,030 --> 00:55:45,710
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the end of it.

794
00:55:46,931 --> 00:55:49,393
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, because the method says only one thing.

795
00:55:49,453 --> 00:55:51,134
[SPEAKER_02]: It says the body is changing.

796
00:55:51,214 --> 00:55:53,056
[SPEAKER_02]: This incontinence may be taken.

797
00:55:54,176 --> 00:55:55,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, they say the body is changing.

798
00:55:55,858 --> 00:55:58,760
[SPEAKER_01]: In that sense, it's constantly changing, like a constant flow.

799
00:56:00,021 --> 00:56:01,142
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not true.

800
00:56:02,094 --> 00:56:06,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's changing on one level, but on another level, it's still the same body.

801
00:56:07,618 --> 00:56:13,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if the body is constantly changing, every second becoming different body, you wouldn't be able to refer to it as the body.

802
00:56:14,024 --> 00:56:16,326
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be able to use the same designation for it.

803
00:56:16,986 --> 00:56:18,968
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that you can say it's still the same body.

804
00:56:19,863 --> 00:56:26,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Means that change that the Buddha talks about is slightly more complex than just saying it's all impermanent.

805
00:56:27,007 --> 00:56:33,151
[SPEAKER_01]: And modern science has proven that atoms and electrons are constantly changing, so everything is in a constant change.

806
00:56:34,432 --> 00:56:37,373
[SPEAKER_01]: If everything is constantly changing, there is no change at all.

807
00:56:38,534 --> 00:56:43,957
[SPEAKER_01]: Because in order for change to be perceived, there has to be a part that's not changing.

808
00:56:44,537 --> 00:56:46,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you can't say it's changing.

809
00:56:46,318 --> 00:56:47,439
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no reference point.

810
00:56:49,685 --> 00:56:53,907
[SPEAKER_01]: So, saying the body is constantly changing is a contradiction in terms, or a wrong view.

811
00:56:55,307 --> 00:56:59,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's changing on one level, while still remaining the same.

812
00:56:59,729 --> 00:57:03,550
[SPEAKER_01]: So, old Mayukha is the same Mayukha that was young Mayukha.

813
00:57:04,251 --> 00:57:05,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, there is a difference.

814
00:57:06,371 --> 00:57:10,853
[SPEAKER_01]: The body, you know, grew bigger, got older, and so on, but it's still the same body.

815
00:57:11,193 --> 00:57:15,715
[SPEAKER_01]: So, change, or rather, as in the Vedas, called invariant under-transformation.

816
00:57:16,648 --> 00:57:18,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Or means persisting, while changing.

817
00:57:18,789 --> 00:57:21,370
[SPEAKER_01]: Persisting the same, while it's changing on another level.

818
00:57:21,750 --> 00:57:31,133
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, Venerable Yāngyur, he said, like, eh... he was looking at the pen, at the hand, at holding the pen, and this is something that, eh... is it same or different?

819
00:57:31,353 --> 00:57:36,595
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it the same, that the thing, he was speaking, like, body's changing in form and something?

820
00:57:37,015 --> 00:57:39,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, well, I don't really remember that context, but I think...

821
00:57:39,996 --> 00:57:45,718
[SPEAKER_02]: He said, like, like, eh... he was looking at the pen, that he was holding the pen in his hand.

822
00:57:46,491 --> 00:57:49,352
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it the same or different?

823
00:57:49,712 --> 00:57:57,694
[SPEAKER_02]: I remember that Venerable Bhārata, he explained, like, that the ship, that there's some part of the ship that is gradually replacing.

824
00:57:58,014 --> 00:58:00,235
[SPEAKER_02]: And is it the same ship or another?

825
00:58:00,395 --> 00:58:01,195
[SPEAKER_01]: I see what you mean.

826
00:58:01,215 --> 00:58:02,335
[SPEAKER_01]: That was slightly different, though.

827
00:58:02,535 --> 00:58:03,675
[SPEAKER_01]: I think there was a different context.

828
00:58:03,695 --> 00:58:05,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I was talking about the pen.

829
00:58:05,576 --> 00:58:09,717
[SPEAKER_01]: It was about the intention to write the letter, the intention to go with the meaning and so on.

830
00:58:09,777 --> 00:58:13,498
[SPEAKER_01]: But, in a way, that's what I was telling you.

831
00:58:15,192 --> 00:58:22,214
[SPEAKER_01]: Maybe last week, I don't remember when it was, when I said, when you designate a thing, that designation is part of a thing.

832
00:58:23,934 --> 00:58:31,296
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you refer to something as a ship, you designate a certain collection of things as a ship.

833
00:58:32,757 --> 00:58:39,939
[SPEAKER_01]: So, sometimes you can change parts so much, that it becomes a different ship.

834
00:58:41,179 --> 00:58:44,180
[SPEAKER_01]: But if your designation was on the level of a ship,

835
00:58:45,028 --> 00:58:46,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Means it still stays the same.

836
00:58:46,829 --> 00:58:50,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Because although it's all different parts, it's still a ship.

837
00:58:50,972 --> 00:58:56,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, if you start putting car parts onto a ship, and then you turn it into a car, you can't say it's a ship anymore.

838
00:58:57,816 --> 00:58:58,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, oh, it's a car now.

839
00:58:59,257 --> 00:59:00,197
[SPEAKER_01]: So it has changed.

840
00:59:01,058 --> 00:59:02,259
[SPEAKER_01]: But that change is external.

841
00:59:03,799 --> 00:59:08,662
[SPEAKER_01]: That change has nothing to do with the impermanence of determinations that the Buddha is talking about.

842
00:59:10,470 --> 00:59:14,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Sabha, saṅkhara, anicca is nothing to do with that, because this is just something you observe with your senses.

843
00:59:15,672 --> 00:59:18,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, the curtains have faded, the door is broken.

844
00:59:19,294 --> 00:59:19,954
[SPEAKER_01]: It's irrelevant.

845
00:59:21,574 --> 00:59:26,196
[SPEAKER_01]: What is not irrelevant is your mood in regard to it, your feelings in regard to that.

846
00:59:26,797 --> 00:59:33,779
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if that door was a very important door, given to you by a very important person, handcrafted, it would be a very valuable door.

847
00:59:34,160 --> 00:59:37,701
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it gets broken, you suffer, because the whole significance is affected by it.

848
00:59:38,840 --> 00:59:40,622
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you were attached to it, in a way.

849
00:59:41,543 --> 00:59:48,150
[SPEAKER_01]: But saying that the door is constantly changing, makes no difference to the fact that it is significant and it means a lot to me.

850
00:59:50,573 --> 00:59:58,582
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether it's changing or not, your... the significance of the door or of any object that you're attached to, remains unaffected.

851
00:59:59,306 --> 01:00:02,627
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can be trying to repeat, no, no, it's all changing, it's all changing, it's all changing.

852
01:00:02,908 --> 01:00:04,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Still, it means a lot to you.

853
01:00:04,768 --> 01:00:10,331
[SPEAKER_01]: And by saying, it's all changing, it's all changing, you're basically trying to deny the fact that it means a lot to you.

854
01:00:11,351 --> 01:00:14,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Instead of taking responsibility, that it means a lot to you.

855
01:00:15,793 --> 01:00:26,898
[SPEAKER_01]: And through accepting responsibility for different amounts of meanings, so to speak, different significance, like, I don't care much about this sheet, but I care about a water filter, because it's more important to me.

856
01:00:29,488 --> 01:00:30,288
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's normal.

857
01:00:30,308 --> 01:00:32,210
[SPEAKER_01]: That's different levels of intentionality.

858
01:00:32,910 --> 01:00:34,811
[SPEAKER_01]: But me trying... it's all changing, it's all changing.

859
01:00:34,851 --> 01:00:38,613
[SPEAKER_01]: It's trying to deny all of that, thinking that's the escape from it.

860
01:00:38,693 --> 01:00:39,233
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not.

861
01:00:40,054 --> 01:00:44,936
[SPEAKER_01]: The escape is... first step is to admit that there are things that mean differently to you.

862
01:00:45,957 --> 01:00:50,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Something... sometimes something means more, sometimes it means less.

863
01:00:51,520 --> 01:00:56,883
[SPEAKER_01]: What you want to accept that responsibility for the different significance and then overcome it.

864
01:00:58,688 --> 01:01:03,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Or rather, become stronger than the strongest significance you're experiencing in the world.

865
01:01:04,952 --> 01:01:06,033
[SPEAKER_01]: You're feminine, usually.

866
01:01:08,774 --> 01:01:12,717
[SPEAKER_01]: That's like... that's something like everybody's usually attached.

867
01:01:13,217 --> 01:01:17,520
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't help but being attached to your family, to your children.

868
01:01:17,580 --> 01:01:17,820
[SPEAKER_01]: So...

869
01:01:18,416 --> 01:01:19,217
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to deny it.

870
01:01:19,497 --> 01:01:22,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can deny it, but it won't free you from the attachment.

871
01:01:22,600 --> 01:01:25,502
[SPEAKER_01]: It will just cause denial, saying, that's not really my child.

872
01:01:25,562 --> 01:01:26,563
[SPEAKER_01]: It's constantly changing.

873
01:01:26,723 --> 01:01:27,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is constantly changing.

874
01:01:27,844 --> 01:01:28,565
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's your child.

875
01:01:30,006 --> 01:01:32,348
[SPEAKER_01]: And he's changing while remaining your child.

876
01:01:33,269 --> 01:01:34,029
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how things work.

877
01:01:34,850 --> 01:01:46,380
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to be able to fully accept responsibility for death-freeing your child, for all the feelings you have, fully accept responsibility, and then become stronger than that responsibility.

878
01:01:47,208 --> 01:01:52,213
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when that child is taken away from you, one way or the other, your mind will not go with it.

879
01:01:52,934 --> 01:01:53,114
[SPEAKER_02]: Hmm.

880
01:01:53,795 --> 01:02:00,641
[SPEAKER_01]: Not through denying that that was your child, but through becoming stronger than any significance you can gather from the world.

881
01:02:01,562 --> 01:02:02,864
[SPEAKER_02]: Become stronger, isn't it?

882
01:02:03,184 --> 01:02:04,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Means not being moved by it.

883
01:02:05,546 --> 01:02:10,251
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if this door is very important to me, it was a gift made, whatever,

884
01:02:11,272 --> 01:02:14,915
[SPEAKER_01]: I acknowledge all that and I fully accept that and full responsibility.

885
01:02:15,015 --> 01:02:19,038
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, if somebody asked, does this door mean more than that room?

886
01:02:19,078 --> 01:02:23,842
[SPEAKER_01]: I would say, yes, it's more important and it was a gift by a good friend or whatever else.

887
01:02:24,182 --> 01:02:26,604
[SPEAKER_01]: He made it specially for me and made lots of effort.

888
01:02:28,446 --> 01:02:30,287
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a... it's real as such.

889
01:02:30,407 --> 01:02:37,753
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's no point in... there's no reason to deny that, unless I believe the denial of that would free me from my attachment to it.

890
01:02:38,293 --> 01:02:38,834
[SPEAKER_01]: But it won't.

891
01:02:39,593 --> 01:02:47,638
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to free myself from the attachment of the important external object, is through overcoming the significance of the external object without denying it.

892
01:02:48,839 --> 01:02:54,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to admit that it's important and then become more stronger than that importance.

893
01:02:55,983 --> 01:03:01,046
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the importance is taken away, when the thing that is important is taken away, you're not affected.

894
01:03:03,486 --> 01:03:08,891
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as the mundane right view says, to repeat again, there is mother, there is father.

895
01:03:08,951 --> 01:03:12,795
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't go around saying, oh, no, it's just a collection of atoms and everything is constantly changing.

896
01:03:12,815 --> 01:03:14,676
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no mother, there is no father, there is nothing.

897
01:03:15,057 --> 01:03:16,158
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what Goenka said.

898
01:03:16,618 --> 01:03:19,401
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means Goenka is not even a mundane right view.

899
01:03:20,662 --> 01:03:21,523
[SPEAKER_01]: And he was just one of them.

900
01:03:21,643 --> 01:03:25,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Really, there's so many... like saying the same things.

901
01:03:26,227 --> 01:03:29,370
[SPEAKER_02]: To become stronger, to overcome enough, you need some understanding.

902
01:03:30,821 --> 01:03:32,702
[SPEAKER_02]: Understanding what?

903
01:03:33,582 --> 01:03:39,424
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding that actually, no matter how important it is, it was inaccessible to you from the beginning.

904
01:03:40,284 --> 01:03:41,965
[SPEAKER_01]: You could have not owned it from the start.

905
01:03:42,545 --> 01:03:47,586
[SPEAKER_01]: The only reason you owned it, the only reason you were attached to it, is because you didn't know that it's impossible to own it.

906
01:03:48,567 --> 01:03:51,587
[SPEAKER_01]: When you know it's impossible to own, it's like a relief.

907
01:03:51,948 --> 01:03:56,149
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, it's an important door, but it doesn't mean that I'll suffer if I lose it.

908
01:03:58,228 --> 01:04:01,950
[SPEAKER_01]: Nor does it mean that I will be all carried away if I get another one.

909
01:04:03,830 --> 01:04:12,234
[SPEAKER_02]: Suppose attachment to child, in that case, that you understand, not... not mine, it means you're actually... how to understand it?

910
01:04:12,254 --> 01:04:25,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, again, first you have to accept it as yours, accept full responsibility for it, for all the feelings you have, and then by becoming stronger than that, you can experience, eh...

911
01:04:26,825 --> 01:04:28,106
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, a form of dishonor.

912
01:04:28,286 --> 01:04:34,570
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not denying the significance of a child, or significance of a parent, or a mother or a father.

913
01:04:35,151 --> 01:04:41,115
[SPEAKER_01]: But means, you develop yourself further than that significance.

914
01:04:41,235 --> 01:04:42,396
[SPEAKER_01]: Further than the significance.

915
01:04:43,156 --> 01:04:49,180
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when the significant object disappears, eventually, means you're not... you won't be taken by it.

916
01:04:50,621 --> 01:04:52,522
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason, again, why...

917
01:04:55,283 --> 01:05:01,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Anicca from the sūtras is different than anicca that's being taught as taught in the sūtras by Goenka and others.

918
01:05:01,489 --> 01:05:05,633
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because you don't actually have to wait for things to cease.

919
01:05:05,893 --> 01:05:10,798
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to see their ending in order to see anicca.

920
01:05:10,818 --> 01:05:13,640
[SPEAKER_01]: You know them that they are impermanent while they're in front of you.

921
01:05:14,982 --> 01:05:15,222
[SPEAKER_02]: Why?

922
01:05:16,023 --> 01:05:17,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they are impermanent.

923
01:05:17,244 --> 01:05:17,584
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

924
01:05:17,884 --> 01:05:20,507
[SPEAKER_01]: Why they're impermanent is because they have arisen on their own.

925
01:05:21,028 --> 01:05:23,490
[SPEAKER_01]: That's quite a difficult point for me to understand.

926
01:05:23,650 --> 01:05:25,492
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a difficult point and it's a different point.

927
01:05:26,032 --> 01:05:28,654
[SPEAKER_01]: Because usually people go, ah, see, see, this tree got destroyed.

928
01:05:28,714 --> 01:05:29,735
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything is impermanent.

929
01:05:30,295 --> 01:05:30,476
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

930
01:05:30,756 --> 01:05:38,102
[SPEAKER_01]: Actually, what you're experiencing right there and there is the positive experience of a destruction of a tree.

931
01:05:39,223 --> 01:05:43,506
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a present experience, which means, actually, nothing exists.

932
01:05:43,586 --> 01:05:45,067
[SPEAKER_01]: There is another experience right there.

933
01:05:45,928 --> 01:05:48,090
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're concerned about things in the world,

934
01:05:49,298 --> 01:05:53,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, yes, the destruction of the tree would mean impermanence.

935
01:05:53,740 --> 01:05:54,240
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't.

936
01:05:54,820 --> 01:06:01,303
[SPEAKER_01]: The impermanence from the suttas refers to the impermanence, impermanent nature of your experience, of your five aggregates.

937
01:06:01,983 --> 01:06:06,324
[SPEAKER_01]: But unless the moment when you die, you won't experience their ending.

938
01:06:07,445 --> 01:06:12,767
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means anicca from the suttas is different than anicca that you can observe with your senses.

939
01:06:13,593 --> 01:06:18,255
[SPEAKER_01]: And it is different, because if it weren't, you wouldn't need a Buddha to teach anicca and anatta.

940
01:06:18,555 --> 01:06:23,156
[SPEAKER_01]: You could just see it yourself, with your eyes, or hear it with your ears.

941
01:06:23,957 --> 01:06:24,917
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes, it's impermanent.

942
01:06:24,957 --> 01:06:25,437
[SPEAKER_01]: I saw it.

943
01:06:27,578 --> 01:06:39,762
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when you see a destruction of a house, again, experience of seeing a destruction of a house has arisen, which means there's no impermanence there.

944
01:06:41,288 --> 01:06:44,951
[SPEAKER_01]: It's only relative impermanence of a certain house changing its shape.

945
01:06:46,352 --> 01:06:51,757
[SPEAKER_01]: From being a useful arranged shape, useful for inhabiting, now it's a less useful shape of a pile of rubble.

946
01:06:53,018 --> 01:07:00,844
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still something you're perceiving, so you just perceive a different perception that you externally explain to yourself as change.

947
01:07:04,247 --> 01:07:04,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see what I mean?

948
01:07:05,548 --> 01:07:05,808
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

949
01:07:06,328 --> 01:07:06,549
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

950
01:07:07,870 --> 01:07:08,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, ask a question then.

951
01:07:10,317 --> 01:07:13,498
[SPEAKER_02]: You said, you said, what, what, what, what, what has arisen?

952
01:07:13,898 --> 01:07:14,738
[SPEAKER_02]: It is impoverished.

953
01:07:14,818 --> 01:07:15,578
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, what do you mean?

954
01:07:15,618 --> 01:07:16,838
[SPEAKER_02]: I actually don't understand.

955
01:07:16,858 --> 01:07:19,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, like, again, let's take the example of a house.

956
01:07:19,379 --> 01:07:20,139
[SPEAKER_01]: You look at the house.

957
01:07:20,719 --> 01:07:22,580
[SPEAKER_01]: You have the experience of seeing a house.

958
01:07:23,140 --> 01:07:24,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Experience as a whole.

959
01:07:24,720 --> 01:07:27,801
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not just talking about a house, because it's never just a house.

960
01:07:27,841 --> 01:07:30,461
[SPEAKER_01]: You are always in every situation.

961
01:07:30,661 --> 01:07:35,822
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when you think about a situation that you are not present in, you are present in your thoughts.

962
01:07:36,483 --> 01:07:38,163
[SPEAKER_01]: Mentally, that's your situation.

963
01:07:39,368 --> 01:07:40,669
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you cannot leave yourself out.

964
01:07:41,089 --> 01:07:44,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means there is no objective scientific world everybody talks about.

965
01:07:46,050 --> 01:07:48,171
[SPEAKER_01]: The only world you know is the one you can perceive.

966
01:07:50,432 --> 01:07:53,512
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you look at a house, that's the experience of seeing a house.

967
01:07:54,853 --> 01:07:59,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, somebody starts destroying a house, that's the experience of seeing house being destroyed.

968
01:08:00,295 --> 01:08:01,275
[SPEAKER_01]: It's another experience.

969
01:08:03,676 --> 01:08:07,577
[SPEAKER_01]: Impermanence that the Buddha talks about pertains to the experience of any kind.

970
01:08:08,330 --> 01:08:10,972
[SPEAKER_01]: Not of the specific house, a tree or a child.

971
01:08:12,012 --> 01:08:17,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Experience in itself is impermanent, because it has a reason on its own.

972
01:08:19,616 --> 01:08:26,100
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, when you're experiencing the perception of the house, your experience is known as impermanent.

973
01:08:27,401 --> 01:08:33,704
[SPEAKER_01]: As a result of that, any ownership that you have to your house will disappear, even if that house remains standing.

974
01:08:35,107 --> 01:08:40,252
[SPEAKER_01]: So, destroying the house, setting it on fire, makes no difference to the internal impermanence.

975
01:08:40,892 --> 01:08:41,372
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing to do.

976
01:08:41,393 --> 01:08:43,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's another experience out there.

977
01:08:46,057 --> 01:08:56,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Sometimes, having a house being destroyed can possibly remind you of, if you already know it first, not if you don't know it, that actually it was impermanent to begin with.

978
01:08:57,126 --> 01:08:59,108
[SPEAKER_01]: It was impermanent while it was still standing.

979
01:08:59,647 --> 01:09:01,109
[SPEAKER_01]: And look, now it's being destroyed.

980
01:09:01,229 --> 01:09:01,589
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course.

981
01:09:01,790 --> 01:09:02,791
[SPEAKER_01]: It was always in common.

982
01:09:03,392 --> 01:09:07,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Subject to destruction means it can be destroyed, it will be destroyed.

983
01:09:07,997 --> 01:09:11,061
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean necessarily it's being destroyed while I'm staring at it.

984
01:09:14,986 --> 01:09:16,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you see the difference now?

985
01:09:16,347 --> 01:09:22,729
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, actually, whether the house is impermanent or not, if I don't have experience with that, there's no any...

986
01:09:22,969 --> 01:09:24,069
[SPEAKER_01]: It's irrelevant.

987
01:09:24,109 --> 01:09:24,890
[SPEAKER_01]: It's irrelevant, yeah.

988
01:09:25,370 --> 01:09:39,034
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only reason people use, like they say, western science, or they say, oh, everything is impermanent, just observe sensations, everything is constantly changing, is basically to deny the responsibility for it, believing that's the escape.

989
01:09:39,594 --> 01:09:40,174
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not.

990
01:09:41,175 --> 01:09:42,595
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a completely wrong direction.

991
01:09:44,382 --> 01:09:49,203
[SPEAKER_01]: If you want to escape it, you first want to admit that the thing means something to you or not.

992
01:09:51,163 --> 01:09:56,224
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, without abandoning that responsibility, then you can see it as impermanent or not.

993
01:09:56,784 --> 01:10:01,845
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't have to witness or have it destroyed in order to prove to yourself that it was impermanent.

994
01:10:02,806 --> 01:10:08,487
[SPEAKER_01]: Meaning, if you needed to prove to yourself externally, means you were never sure about it internally, which means there is no knowledge.

995
01:10:11,312 --> 01:10:15,833
[SPEAKER_02]: Why Buddha taught to observe the body?

996
01:10:15,853 --> 01:10:16,313
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't.

997
01:10:17,253 --> 01:10:17,453
[SPEAKER_01]: No?

998
01:10:17,713 --> 01:10:18,234
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

999
01:10:18,294 --> 01:10:18,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Never like that.

1000
01:10:19,394 --> 01:10:23,855
[SPEAKER_01]: He would say, you observe parts of the body, like lungs or whatever, different organs.

1001
01:10:23,995 --> 01:10:27,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Take them out of the context of my body, my strong, healthy body.

1002
01:10:28,476 --> 01:10:32,417
[SPEAKER_01]: So to experience disgust, dispassion, in regard to that body.

1003
01:10:33,097 --> 01:10:37,138
[SPEAKER_01]: If you just think of a pair of lungs, it's quite disgusting.

1004
01:10:38,623 --> 01:10:40,165
[SPEAKER_01]: That's exactly what I have in me.

1005
01:10:40,785 --> 01:10:49,431
[SPEAKER_01]: If you think about intestines, and skin, hair, think about that, it will make you experience dispassion, because it's not beautiful.

1006
01:10:50,291 --> 01:10:54,914
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't teach sweeping sensations and be aware of your kneecaps or whatever else.

1007
01:10:55,575 --> 01:10:59,838
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it was it was a contemplation, bringing those things to mind, so to experience dispassion.

1008
01:11:00,378 --> 01:11:06,702
[SPEAKER_01]: If you do it long enough, then even when you don't have those things in your mind, just being aware of your body will be seen as those

1009
01:11:09,023 --> 01:11:09,623
[SPEAKER_01]: Ugly things.

1010
01:11:09,843 --> 01:11:14,485
[SPEAKER_02]: It's very interesting.

1011
01:11:16,186 --> 01:11:23,289
[SPEAKER_02]: I think many people, like, in Thailand, I heard, like, many people, they have a body contemplation and, like, a dead, dead, dead body.

1012
01:11:23,329 --> 01:11:25,310
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, that's what I'm trying to say.

1013
01:11:25,350 --> 01:11:26,190
[SPEAKER_01]: It's for that reason.

1014
01:11:26,290 --> 01:11:26,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1015
01:11:26,731 --> 01:11:27,611
[SPEAKER_02]: As experience.

1016
01:11:27,651 --> 01:11:32,113
[SPEAKER_01]: Because usually people don't think about death, don't want to think about death, don't see death.

1017
01:11:32,553 --> 01:11:37,335
[SPEAKER_01]: So, seeing a dead body or things of that nature will over remind you

1018
01:11:38,744 --> 01:11:39,604
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not be...

1019
01:11:39,664 --> 01:11:40,604
[SPEAKER_01]: I cannot escape this.

1020
01:11:40,884 --> 01:11:41,785
[SPEAKER_01]: This will happen to me.

1021
01:11:42,945 --> 01:11:46,725
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you experience dispassion towards your life, towards life of others.

1022
01:11:46,805 --> 01:11:49,446
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, oh, it will all end like this.

1023
01:11:49,646 --> 01:11:54,407
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a reminder of something that kind of everybody knows at the back of their mind.

1024
01:11:55,407 --> 01:11:57,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, it's not... Again, there is no wisdom.

1025
01:11:58,927 --> 01:12:08,049
[SPEAKER_01]: People can can go a bit funny in regard to it, because it can be too much of a shock or something, and then they can go mad, as they did in the suttas, when they really

1026
01:12:08,829 --> 01:12:10,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Practiced hard contemplation of death.

1027
01:12:12,051 --> 01:12:13,272
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha went out for the retreat.

1028
01:12:14,073 --> 01:12:15,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Three months later, he came back.

1029
01:12:15,214 --> 01:12:17,215
[SPEAKER_01]: He's like, maybe half of the monks left.

1030
01:12:17,816 --> 01:12:18,676
[SPEAKER_01]: He's like, what happened to the rest?

1031
01:12:18,696 --> 01:12:25,041
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, well, they were jumping off the cliffs and stuff, because they were... And he said, okay, well, in that case, practice mindfulness of breathing.

1032
01:12:25,061 --> 01:12:27,943
[SPEAKER_01]: There won't result in such extreme actions.

1033
01:12:28,744 --> 01:12:37,771
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, if somebody does maraṇasati correctly, they can become arahant very quickly, because it's a thorough, direct, forceful dispassion.

1034
01:12:38,680 --> 01:12:39,921
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have to be able to hold it.

1035
01:12:40,061 --> 01:12:43,542
[SPEAKER_01]: If not, you know, you can go a bit mad.

1036
01:12:44,463 --> 01:12:44,743
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1037
01:12:44,823 --> 01:12:45,463
[SPEAKER_02]: I did a list.

1038
01:12:45,923 --> 01:12:46,784
[SPEAKER_02]: It's very interesting.

1039
01:12:46,844 --> 01:12:50,485
[SPEAKER_02]: Attachment of the child also, it means that as experience.

1040
01:12:50,605 --> 01:12:52,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's real.

1041
01:12:52,846 --> 01:12:53,706
[SPEAKER_01]: It's real as such.

1042
01:12:54,587 --> 01:12:57,268
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, people think they can deal with attachment by denying it.

1043
01:12:57,348 --> 01:12:59,649
[SPEAKER_01]: By saying, well, it's not really.

1044
01:12:59,669 --> 01:13:00,449
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just atoms.

1045
01:13:00,489 --> 01:13:01,730
[SPEAKER_01]: It's molecules.

1046
01:13:01,830 --> 01:13:04,651
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't need to see, like, the child's body is changing.

1047
01:13:04,751 --> 01:13:05,392
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

1048
01:13:05,452 --> 01:13:06,232
[SPEAKER_02]: It's irrelevant.

1049
01:13:07,585 --> 01:13:09,506
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to accept that it's your child.

1050
01:13:10,106 --> 01:13:13,328
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to accept the responsibility for your feelings.

1051
01:13:13,368 --> 01:13:14,288
[SPEAKER_01]: You must not deny them.

1052
01:13:15,229 --> 01:13:19,511
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to accept the weight, the significance of having a child.

1053
01:13:20,612 --> 01:13:21,912
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you can overcome.

1054
01:13:22,193 --> 01:13:24,254
[SPEAKER_02]: Then, the experience is impermanent.

1055
01:13:25,014 --> 01:13:25,274
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1056
01:13:27,055 --> 01:13:28,576
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the impermanence pertains.

1057
01:13:29,016 --> 01:13:34,319
[SPEAKER_02]: Experience is impermanent means that experience is changing?

1058
01:13:34,559 --> 01:13:36,600
[SPEAKER_01]: No, means it will come to an end.

1059
01:13:37,850 --> 01:13:39,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't see the end, necessarily.

1060
01:13:39,712 --> 01:13:43,094
[SPEAKER_02]: That's very impassive little point, that you don't see the end.

1061
01:13:43,234 --> 01:13:48,618
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Sūtra says, whatever has the nature of arising, has the nature of ceasing.

1062
01:13:49,439 --> 01:13:55,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're not witnessing the ceasing, just in its nature, it will be affected by cessation.

1063
01:13:55,404 --> 01:13:57,525
[SPEAKER_01]: It will cease to come to an end.

1064
01:13:59,006 --> 01:13:59,307
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

1065
01:13:59,707 --> 01:14:00,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it has arisen.

1066
01:14:01,468 --> 01:14:03,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Because its nature has manifested.

1067
01:14:03,209 --> 01:14:05,051
[SPEAKER_01]: For that reason, it will have to go.

1068
01:14:05,872 --> 01:14:06,332
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

1069
01:14:07,888 --> 01:14:13,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because molecules are changing, not because somebody can come and destroy it, just because it has appeared.

1070
01:14:14,534 --> 01:14:16,836
[SPEAKER_02]: Are sankhāderas nibbāna and nippāna?

1071
01:14:17,476 --> 01:14:18,197
[SPEAKER_02]: Is it true?

1072
01:14:18,697 --> 01:14:18,917
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

1073
01:14:19,738 --> 01:14:19,958
[SPEAKER_02]: No?

1074
01:14:20,439 --> 01:14:31,448
[SPEAKER_01]: No, because permanent and impermanent doesn't apply to nibbāna, because nibbāna cannot even be... Any designations apply to things, and nibbāna means surpassing all that.

1075
01:14:31,568 --> 01:14:36,052
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, using those designations of permanent and impermanent to nibbāna doesn't make sense.

1076
01:14:39,290 --> 01:14:49,393
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is a, I think, it's Chula Veda, when Bhikkhuni Damodara was answering the questions of her ex-husband, and he was asking, where does this lead?

1077
01:14:49,433 --> 01:14:50,093
[SPEAKER_01]: Where does this lead?

1078
01:14:50,153 --> 01:14:52,134
[SPEAKER_01]: And they came to a point and said, well, that leads to nibbāna.

1079
01:14:52,534 --> 01:14:54,134
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, well, where does nibbāna lead?

1080
01:14:54,394 --> 01:14:55,035
[SPEAKER_01]: What's nibbāna?

1081
01:14:55,055 --> 01:14:59,396
[SPEAKER_01]: He's like, well, the point of your questioning has lost meaning then.

1082
01:14:59,476 --> 01:14:59,896
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

1083
01:15:00,096 --> 01:15:04,657
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't ask that, because all the designations you use cannot be applied to nibbāna.

1084
01:15:04,677 --> 01:15:06,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, any answer I give you won't make any sense

1085
01:15:07,618 --> 01:15:09,159
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, it could be completely misperceived.

1086
01:15:10,280 --> 01:15:17,825
[SPEAKER_00]: In Sweden, there's actually at least 32 designations of nibbāna, like describing another show.

1087
01:15:18,405 --> 01:15:18,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, right, yeah.

1088
01:15:18,945 --> 01:15:19,446
[SPEAKER_01]: That's an example.

1089
01:15:19,466 --> 01:15:21,547
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, I'm talking about nicca.

1090
01:15:21,707 --> 01:15:22,107
Nicca.

1091
01:15:22,207 --> 01:15:22,648
[SPEAKER_01]: Permanent.

1092
01:15:23,448 --> 01:15:25,289
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I guess, I haven't read that sutra, but...

1093
01:15:26,750 --> 01:15:32,674
[SPEAKER_01]: If you mean, in terms of nibbāna, the other show, uh, cessation of universal delusion,

1094
01:15:33,440 --> 01:15:47,431
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you say in that sense, as Hiriku just told me about this sutta, I can see that if you say in that sense, oh, it's permanent, from the point of view of the impermanent that you are living in, then, yeah.

1095
01:15:47,951 --> 01:15:50,313
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't say it's permanent, it lasts forever.

1096
01:15:51,774 --> 01:15:56,558
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if something... Usually, if people say permanent, they mean eternal.

1097
01:15:58,680 --> 01:15:59,720
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is a difference there.

1098
01:16:03,640 --> 01:16:09,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Eternal means, basically, when things are taken as self, it will last forever.

1099
01:16:09,424 --> 01:16:12,527
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's usually the sense of permanence.

1100
01:16:12,727 --> 01:16:13,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Like a soul or something.

1101
01:16:13,728 --> 01:16:18,731
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if somebody said nibbāna is permanent, by quoting this sutta, that's okay.

1102
01:16:18,751 --> 01:16:23,255
[SPEAKER_01]: But then it should be clarified that it's in the context, from the point of view of the impermanent.

1103
01:16:24,336 --> 01:16:28,119
[SPEAKER_01]: But if they say nibbāna is permanent, means once you enter it, it lasts forever.

1104
01:16:28,159 --> 01:16:28,919
[SPEAKER_01]: That makes no sense.

1105
01:16:29,299 --> 01:16:31,141
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the idea of lasting and time

1106
01:16:31,640 --> 01:16:33,821
[SPEAKER_01]: cannot pertain to timeless nibbāna.

1107
01:16:35,982 --> 01:16:42,466
[SPEAKER_01]: So, permanent, as pertaining... referring to time, usually, cannot pertain to the timeless nibbāna.

1108
01:16:42,966 --> 01:16:43,767
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't make sense.

1109
01:16:44,427 --> 01:16:49,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you use it as epithet to describe it, in which case you should probably clarify it as such.

1110
01:16:49,950 --> 01:16:50,790
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not right to say.

1111
01:16:50,810 --> 01:16:59,895
[SPEAKER_02]: Saṃsāra is permanent only based on determination, that is, because... Again, it's not... it's not permanent.

1112
01:17:00,055 --> 01:17:00,636
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just that

1113
01:17:01,462 --> 01:17:06,105
[SPEAKER_01]: It's going to be occurring for as long as you don't see the nature of it and escape.

1114
01:17:06,285 --> 01:17:06,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

1115
01:17:07,446 --> 01:17:13,870
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, again, saying permanent or impermanent cannot really designate that correctly.

1116
01:17:16,012 --> 01:17:25,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless you're using to inspire somebody, to say them, yeah, from your point of view of this all impermanence, this is the permanent, this is the stable, this is the free from suffering.

1117
01:17:25,558 --> 01:17:25,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

1118
01:17:27,139 --> 01:17:38,609
[SPEAKER_01]: But in terms of, if you use permanent as a designation of time, last forever, it doesn't work like that.

1119
01:17:38,629 --> 01:17:42,452
[SPEAKER_02]: What, what is the reason it is impermanent?

1120
01:17:42,512 --> 01:17:44,294
[SPEAKER_02]: It is just a law of nature.

1121
01:17:44,614 --> 01:17:45,935
[SPEAKER_02]: You just must understand it.

1122
01:17:46,096 --> 01:17:46,456
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry?

1123
01:17:46,976 --> 01:17:49,839
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, what, what is the reason that they experience it?

1124
01:17:49,919 --> 01:17:51,060
[SPEAKER_02]: It is impermanent.

1125
01:17:51,499 --> 01:17:57,063
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a serious... sometimes... because usually we don't see the beginning and the end.

1126
01:17:57,183 --> 01:17:59,605
[SPEAKER_02]: It's quite difficult to see the... You don't.

1127
01:18:00,766 --> 01:18:04,449
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to see them correctly, one has a reason is impermanent, because he has a reason.

1128
01:18:04,509 --> 01:18:07,091
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you have to accept it that what is the reason is real.

1129
01:18:08,172 --> 01:18:11,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if it's a fantasy in your thoughts, it's real as such.

1130
01:18:12,655 --> 01:18:14,436
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you have to accept responsibility for it.

1131
01:18:15,417 --> 01:18:17,819
[SPEAKER_01]: And then through that, you can eventually see it.

1132
01:18:18,488 --> 01:18:21,369
[SPEAKER_01]: As impermanent for the reasons of manifesting by itself.

1133
01:18:23,870 --> 01:18:31,393
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you kind of cut short and try to do it before, basically means you'll be... it will become a part of denial.

1134
01:18:32,834 --> 01:18:44,158
[SPEAKER_02]: I feel, I think, that the only way to show the impermanence clearly is, suppose, it's like two Christian people who never accept dharma and buddhism.

1135
01:18:44,615 --> 01:18:56,780
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, then, what we can really clearly explain, just the things are impermanent, and it's quite difficult to explain clearly, but the, like, attention, now, attention changes, the object.

1136
01:18:56,800 --> 01:18:59,541
[SPEAKER_02]: That's the only way to explain, like, impermanence.

1137
01:18:59,622 --> 01:19:01,983
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't think they'll get it like that.

1138
01:19:02,723 --> 01:19:14,248
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, first, they would... they have to be willing, whoever, not just Christians, or whoever you're talking, even Buddhists, actually, they have to be willing to hear, they have to be willing to accept that they don't necessarily know,

1139
01:19:15,490 --> 01:19:19,532
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if they think they already know it, whatever you say will fall on deaf ears.

1140
01:19:19,592 --> 01:19:24,135
[SPEAKER_01]: So, saying everything is changing, we'll go, OK, so what?

1141
01:19:25,756 --> 01:19:34,001
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it's closer, probably, to say, well, the fact that you will die, the fact that you don't really know what's after your life, you can't be sure.

1142
01:19:34,601 --> 01:19:42,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you really want to spend your life based on a mere belief, a mere faith, in an imaginary friend or something like that?

1143
01:19:42,565 --> 01:19:43,226
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't know.

1144
01:19:43,926 --> 01:19:44,847
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot verify.

1145
01:19:45,784 --> 01:19:50,346
[SPEAKER_01]: That God exists as such, that heaven exists as such, that Jesus exists as such, that you'll be saved for sure.

1146
01:19:50,646 --> 01:19:51,487
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you be sure?

1147
01:19:52,587 --> 01:20:03,553
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you do everything rightly for the rest of your life, as Jesus prescribed, or whoever, in the Bible, you still cannot be 100% sure that that will be the outcome.

1148
01:20:03,693 --> 01:20:14,618
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your entire life, all of your actions, all of your choices will revolve founded, based upon, rooted in faith, in just blind faith.

1149
01:20:15,521 --> 01:20:16,582
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's unwholesome.

1150
01:20:17,422 --> 01:20:30,268
[SPEAKER_02]: If someone is suffering, suffering, suffering, suffering from sickness, like a terminal, terminal ill, then, eh, if, if they, they, like, ask us, then, how to be free from suffering, of course, in the case of Uttarachandra?

1151
01:20:30,308 --> 01:20:33,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I think it might, it might, it might be a bit late for them at that point.

1152
01:20:33,770 --> 01:20:37,371
[SPEAKER_02]: If we just tell them that the things have arisen, it is impermanent.

1153
01:20:37,391 --> 01:20:40,113
[SPEAKER_02]: But they feel like the pain is there every day.

1154
01:20:40,313 --> 01:20:43,834
[SPEAKER_01]: If they've been practicing throughout their life, then, yeah, you could remind them of the d

1155
01:20:45,775 --> 01:20:48,277
[SPEAKER_01]: But if they haven't, you can't say anything.

1156
01:20:48,797 --> 01:20:53,300
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a very difficult point, I think, because even Vedanānyāna really suffered, eh, in sickness.

1157
01:20:53,360 --> 01:20:53,721
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1158
01:20:53,741 --> 01:20:55,802
[SPEAKER_02]: So, after pain, there's pain, there's pain.

1159
01:20:55,822 --> 01:20:58,044
[SPEAKER_02]: Samādhi is real, he's here, he's sick.

1160
01:20:58,064 --> 01:20:58,864
[SPEAKER_02]: Real.

1161
01:20:58,884 --> 01:20:59,845
[SPEAKER_02]: Because pain is everyday.

1162
01:20:59,865 --> 01:21:00,425
[SPEAKER_02]: This is morning.

1163
01:21:00,445 --> 01:21:07,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the most you can say to a person who never practiced, at the moment of their death, is to think about good things they've done in their life.

1164
01:21:08,691 --> 01:21:11,153
[SPEAKER_01]: Provided they were good things that they've done.

1165
01:21:11,833 --> 01:21:12,934
[SPEAKER_01]: If they haven't, well then,

1166
01:21:15,039 --> 01:21:16,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to think good things now.

1167
01:21:16,440 --> 01:21:18,642
[SPEAKER_01]: But, you know, it might be a bit late, but obviously...

1168
01:21:18,962 --> 01:21:22,004
[SPEAKER_02]: When I'm... when I'm sick, that doesn't mean I observe.

1169
01:21:22,024 --> 01:21:26,507
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what only I can see as relief is like attention definitely changes.

1170
01:21:26,527 --> 01:21:28,528
[SPEAKER_02]: Because all the time we never think about the pain.

1171
01:21:28,548 --> 01:21:30,050
[SPEAKER_02]: Pain is present all the time.

1172
01:21:30,370 --> 01:21:34,292
[SPEAKER_02]: But our... our... we are thinking about many other things.

1173
01:21:34,393 --> 01:21:36,714
[SPEAKER_02]: Our attention changes, the object of thinking.

1174
01:21:36,834 --> 01:21:39,596
[SPEAKER_02]: Only that we went for understanding commonness.

1175
01:21:39,616 --> 01:21:39,756
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

1176
01:21:39,816 --> 01:21:40,357
[SPEAKER_02]: Oh, right.

1177
01:21:40,477 --> 01:21:41,457
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

1178
01:21:41,558 --> 01:21:42,118
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said,

1179
01:21:42,765 --> 01:21:45,987
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to keep witnessing the change to understand impermanence.

1180
01:21:46,947 --> 01:21:55,632
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to recognize independent arising of things, arising on their own, in order to recognize impermanence.

1181
01:21:56,572 --> 01:22:00,354
[SPEAKER_01]: They're not impermanent because they will... they're not impermanent because they're going to come to an end.

1182
01:22:01,215 --> 01:22:04,397
[SPEAKER_01]: They're already impermanent because they have arisen in the first place.

1183
01:22:05,897 --> 01:22:07,378
[SPEAKER_01]: So they will have to come to an end.

1184
01:22:09,596 --> 01:22:11,477
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop worrying about cessation of things.

1185
01:22:11,537 --> 01:22:13,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Start looking in their arising.

1186
01:22:14,358 --> 01:22:16,778
[SPEAKER_01]: If they have arisen, you have no say in it.

1187
01:22:17,699 --> 01:22:19,559
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no say in arising your thoughts.

1188
01:22:20,319 --> 01:22:23,820
[SPEAKER_01]: You have some say in what you're going to attend, once it has arisen.

1189
01:22:25,121 --> 01:22:27,021
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact is, it will always arise on its own.

1190
01:22:28,542 --> 01:22:33,263
[SPEAKER_02]: I heard that Prabhupāda Nārāyaṇī was doing walking meditation on the here earth in prominence.

1191
01:22:33,283 --> 01:22:33,764
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

1192
01:22:33,844 --> 01:22:35,164
[SPEAKER_01]: How to understand?

1193
01:22:39,110 --> 01:22:42,432
[SPEAKER_02]: I simply heard that Padma's eyes need to cease.

1194
01:22:43,633 --> 01:22:46,855
[SPEAKER_02]: It's quite hard to understand.

1195
01:22:47,116 --> 01:22:47,476
[SPEAKER_02]: It is.

1196
01:22:47,716 --> 01:22:49,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you would, you would be a sotāpanna.

1197
01:22:49,357 --> 01:22:54,521
[SPEAKER_02]: But what he kept observing, like an experience, what... Well, he was pondering and reflecting on dhamma.

1198
01:22:55,021 --> 01:22:55,922
[SPEAKER_02]: Dhamma.

1199
01:22:56,242 --> 01:22:57,803
[SPEAKER_01]: Nature of saṅkhara.

1200
01:22:58,824 --> 01:23:01,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Nature of determinations.

1201
01:23:01,065 --> 01:23:01,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Nature of arising.

1202
01:23:05,788 --> 01:23:08,430
[SPEAKER_01]: Three people, and you did an experiment on that.

1203
01:23:08,861 --> 01:23:11,322
[SPEAKER_01]: And you forget that you are the one observing the experiment.

1204
01:23:12,102 --> 01:23:17,443
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you are focused on your experiment, you're going to be able to see yourself experimenting.

1205
01:23:17,523 --> 01:23:20,783
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you see yourself experimenting, you have to... you can stop doing experiments.

1206
01:23:21,384 --> 01:23:22,964
[SPEAKER_01]: You can just see yourself doing whatever.

1207
01:23:24,064 --> 01:23:38,427
[SPEAKER_02]: So, the way you maintain mindfulness means already you see the background, the mood, the... the... the way... how you're feeling, the mental state, the... in the relation to the situation where you... And see how that affects you on a particular level.

1208
01:23:39,320 --> 01:23:42,101
[SPEAKER_01]: What it makes you think, what it makes you choose, what it makes you dwell on.

1209
01:23:42,801 --> 01:23:49,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it makes you dwell on the bad things, because you're in a bad mood, well, you can choose to dwell on not bad things.

1210
01:23:49,923 --> 01:23:52,804
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you can choose to not dwell on bad things.

1211
01:23:52,844 --> 01:23:55,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you dwell on good things, while you're in a bad mood.

1212
01:23:56,045 --> 01:24:00,607
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep dwelling on good things, or more inspiring things, and yeah, you can change that mood.

1213
01:24:01,047 --> 01:24:05,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mood is not completely independent from a particular situation either.

1214
01:24:06,449 --> 01:24:07,209
[SPEAKER_01]: From a particular...

1215
01:24:08,815 --> 01:24:09,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Thoughts or perceptions.

1216
01:24:10,616 --> 01:24:11,977
[SPEAKER_01]: But, obviously, it's on a different level.

1217
01:24:12,577 --> 01:24:14,678
[SPEAKER_01]: They're simultaneously there, but on a different level.

1218
01:24:17,340 --> 01:24:21,442
[SPEAKER_02]: So, I think, exactly, we don't need to know where this body is.

1219
01:24:21,582 --> 01:24:23,824
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not that important, I think, the mood.

1220
01:24:24,024 --> 01:24:27,266
[SPEAKER_02]: So, in that case, this body is now in the court.

1221
01:24:27,286 --> 01:24:28,787
[SPEAKER_02]: It's not that important.

1222
01:24:28,807 --> 01:24:32,849
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to think about the location, but you have to think about you in the situation.

1223
01:24:32,969 --> 01:24:34,510
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is your body in the situation?

1224
01:24:36,493 --> 01:24:45,316
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whether it's you and your kuti, or you're in a tuk-tuk, or you're walking on a path, you, in a certain location, which is what makes the whole situation.

1225
01:24:45,336 --> 01:24:49,577
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't have to be visualizing and thinking about the kuti and the shape of the walls.

1226
01:24:49,857 --> 01:24:52,458
[SPEAKER_01]: You just have to know that you are in that situation.

1227
01:24:54,238 --> 01:24:59,160
[SPEAKER_01]: And the bodily posture is like the first, kind of, first

1228
01:25:00,138 --> 01:25:01,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Designation of your situation.

1229
01:25:01,640 --> 01:25:03,361
[SPEAKER_01]: You're seated in such and such place.

1230
01:25:03,421 --> 01:25:04,682
[SPEAKER_01]: You're standing in such and such.

1231
01:25:04,963 --> 01:25:08,947
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like the most fundamental external output you're in.

1232
01:25:10,468 --> 01:25:18,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Because while you're seated, you can't have intentions of, you know, based on your standing, or based on your walking, or based on you lying down.

1233
01:25:20,057 --> 01:25:24,982
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why being aware of your bodily posture, kind of makes you aware of the whole situation already.

1234
01:25:26,021 --> 01:25:31,866
[SPEAKER_01]: But being aware of your bodily posture as a background, not as a kind of going, observing sensation.

1235
01:25:31,886 --> 01:25:36,530
[SPEAKER_02]: It's actually very difficult that there is a mood, the mental state.

1236
01:25:36,970 --> 01:25:39,493
[SPEAKER_02]: Seeing it as general background is quite hard.

1237
01:25:39,513 --> 01:25:41,134
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes it appears in front.

1238
01:25:41,514 --> 01:25:47,079
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if it appears in front, you know that there's something appearing in front, but there is always a background to it.

1239
01:25:47,760 --> 01:25:51,503
[SPEAKER_01]: So that doesn't mean that you lost the sight of your background, unless you got confused.

1240
01:25:52,160 --> 01:25:57,683
[SPEAKER_02]: Suppose if the mood is... mood is happy or whatever.

1241
01:25:58,263 --> 01:25:59,624
[SPEAKER_02]: It is not in front.

1242
01:25:59,704 --> 01:26:01,725
[SPEAKER_02]: I see it as background.

1243
01:26:03,666 --> 01:26:04,246
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1244
01:26:04,266 --> 01:26:05,667
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say, oh, I am happy.

1245
01:26:06,628 --> 01:26:09,749
[SPEAKER_01]: And that doesn't mean that your back will not change suddenly.

1246
01:26:09,769 --> 01:26:10,670
[SPEAKER_01]: You can still be happy.

1247
01:26:11,210 --> 01:26:14,872
[SPEAKER_01]: But you shouldn't confuse that you saying, I'm happy, is your happiness.

1248
01:26:15,953 --> 01:26:18,534
[SPEAKER_01]: Happiness is the state of mind which is always in the background.

1249
01:26:19,680 --> 01:26:26,325
[SPEAKER_02]: The purpose of the practice is to understand the intention of the... Well, yeah, to overcome it.

1250
01:26:26,946 --> 01:26:29,488
[SPEAKER_02]: To understand it, to overcome it.

1251
01:26:29,528 --> 01:26:41,978
[SPEAKER_01]: By recognizing that things arise on their own, by not being moved by your moods, wholesome or unwholesome, you end up, like, well, you stop appropriating it.

1252
01:26:42,038 --> 01:26:45,300
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, ah, this thing doesn't require sense of self.

1253
01:26:45,320 --> 01:26:47,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense of self becomes part of that mood.

1254
01:26:48,509 --> 01:26:53,855
[SPEAKER_01]: Me just becomes part of that whole picture of background and of general and particular.

1255
01:26:54,916 --> 01:26:58,400
[SPEAKER_01]: And by not knowing general and particular, you were centered on yourself.

1256
01:26:58,921 --> 01:27:03,566
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you recognize this operates without the self, so the center from yourself is removed.

1257
01:27:04,487 --> 01:27:06,750
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the center is not in the self, that's not self.

1258
01:27:12,258 --> 01:27:19,342
[SPEAKER_02]: Actually, the self, if really I believe in self like that, actually, I see clearly, actually, self becomes incommon.

1259
01:27:19,362 --> 01:27:26,647
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, suppose, because where this body is in, actually, according to the situation, where this body is in, actually, the rule changes.

1260
01:27:26,787 --> 01:27:30,529
[SPEAKER_02]: It means, like, it's quite strange that if that is really self, then...

1261
01:27:31,109 --> 01:27:32,891
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't change.

1262
01:27:34,752 --> 01:27:40,235
[SPEAKER_01]: But you have... it's something that needs to be... you have to dwell on it, on and on.

1263
01:27:40,582 --> 01:27:51,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the self is not... you see it once, you have to keep dwelling on it, that it cannot be owned, that it's subject to cessation because it's already there, because you have no say in it.

1264
01:27:52,135 --> 01:27:56,960
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you dwell on it long enough, then you'll stop feeling its mind.

1265
01:27:59,992 --> 01:28:04,953
[SPEAKER_02]: It means there's no meaning that to keep seeing that it's arising intentionally.

1266
01:28:04,973 --> 01:28:09,714
[SPEAKER_02]: It has already... it is the given... given the intention, so it is not mine.

1267
01:28:09,874 --> 01:28:13,055
[SPEAKER_02]: If I keep observing, there's no point of it.

1268
01:28:13,275 --> 01:28:18,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there is no point because if you do it like that, you end up not taking responsibility for it.

1269
01:28:19,296 --> 01:28:25,977
[SPEAKER_01]: First, you have to learn how to always be responsible for that, and then you can start saying, yeah, this is not mine.

1270
01:28:26,367 --> 01:28:28,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Through that responsibility, not before.

1271
01:28:29,148 --> 01:28:30,889
[SPEAKER_01]: So, order of things is important.

1272
01:28:31,629 --> 01:28:33,190
[SPEAKER_01]: All externally might be the same.

1273
01:28:33,230 --> 01:28:35,431
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, this doesn't belong to me.

1274
01:28:35,471 --> 01:28:36,011
[SPEAKER_01]: It depends.

1275
01:28:36,391 --> 01:28:39,932
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether you said it before, you took full responsibility, or after.

1276
01:28:40,913 --> 01:28:48,235
[SPEAKER_01]: If you said before, means you obscure the responsibility, you obscure the feeling, you obscure the karma.

1277
01:28:48,455 --> 01:28:51,257
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you do it after, then, ah, okay.

1278
01:28:51,337 --> 01:28:54,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Without abandoning, which means you don't do it for the reasons

1279
01:28:55,573 --> 01:28:58,854
